INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('57d677e7-c823-43ac-b63e-77bfce481f1d'::uuid,'5531c52e-2f10-49bd-ac07-9933aadc961a'::uuid,11,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Why is Encapsulation important?
By controlling how data and methods are
accessed or modified, encapsulation ensures:
●
Data Integrity: Prevents unauthorized or
accidental changes to data.
●
Security: Sensitive data is hidden from
unauthorized access.
●
Ease of Use: Users of the class do not need
to understand its internal complexities.
●
Code Maintenance: Internal
implementation can change without
impacting external usage.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the significance of encapsulation in object-oriented programming. By regulating access to data and methods, encapsulation provides four key benefits: it maintains data integrity by preventing unauthorized modifications, enhances security by hiding sensitive details, simplifies usability for other developers by masking complexity, and facilitates easier code maintenance by allowing internal changes without affecting external interactions.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. How does encapsulation help prevent accidental or unauthorized changes to data?
2. In what way does encapsulation contribute to the security of sensitive information within a class?
3. Why is a class easier to use for other programmers when encapsulation is applied?
4. How does encapsulation allow for code maintenance without affecting external components?','e22d5e99-c156-4e77-8617-804871b73032'::uuid,NULL,NULL,12,330,'2026-05-08 16:17:02.849884+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('d8fbce2a-4ed2-44e7-ae39-e3cc3bccf5f9'::uuid,'5531c52e-2f10-49bd-ac07-9933aadc961a'::uuid,12,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Core principles of Encapsulation
Data Hiding: The internal state of an object is kept private and can only
be accessed through public methods (getters and setters).
Access Control: Access specifiers are used to control the visibility of
class members:
private: Accessible only within the defining class.
default: Accessible only within the same package.
protected: Accessible within the same package and by subclasses (even
in different packages).
public: Accessible from anywhere (any class, any package, and any
subclass).

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the fundamental concepts of encapsulation: data hiding and access control. Data hiding protects an object''s internal state by making it private and accessible only via public methods like getters and setters. Access control utilizes four specifiers—private, default, protected, and public—to define the visibility of class members across different scopes, including classes, packages, and subclasses. Together, these principles allow developers to restrict access to sensitive data and manage how different parts of a program interact with an object''s data.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. According to the text, how should the internal state of an object be accessed to ensure data hiding?
2. What is the specific difference between ''private'' and ''default'' access specifiers?
3. Under what circumstances can a ''protected'' class member be accessed?
4. Which access specifier offers the least amount of restriction for class members?','a5188014-0742-4bdb-a21a-15702ac47354'::uuid,NULL,NULL,13,379,'2026-05-08 16:17:02.849964+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('c93e10ac-0c30-4f73-af2a-6cb737da1fa5'::uuid,'5531c52e-2f10-49bd-ac07-9933aadc961a'::uuid,13,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Example
public class Person {
// Public: Accessible anywhere
public String name;
// Protected: Accessible within the same package or by subclasses
protected int age;
// Default (Package-private): Accessible only within the same package
String address;
// Private: Accessible only within this class
private String password;
// Constructor to initialize the object
public Person(String name, int age, String address, String password) {
this.name = name;
this.age = age;
this.address = address;
this.password = password;
}
// Getter for password to demonstrate controlled access
public String getPassword() {
return password;
}
14

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment provides a practical Java code example of the Person class to illustrate the four access modifiers: public, protected, default (package-private), and private. It demonstrates how different levels of visibility are applied to class fields and shows the use of a constructor for initialization. Additionally, it highlights the concept of encapsulation by using a public getter method to provide controlled access to a private field.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What are the four access modifiers demonstrated in the Person class example?
2. Which field in the example is only accessible within the Person class itself, and how is it accessed by external users?
3. In this Java example, what is the visibility of the ''address'' field?
4. What is the purpose of the getPassword() method in the context of encapsulation?','d929265c-b332-4d29-8744-7d72f74ecae2'::uuid,NULL,NULL,14,374,'2026-05-08 16:17:02.850021+07');
