INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('2c927a43-52c2-43ea-8bd5-d20351bf05b1'::uuid,'5531c52e-2f10-49bd-ac07-9933aadc961a'::uuid,16,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Constructor in Encapsulation
A constructor is a special function in a class:
●
Same name as the class.
●
Not have a return value.
●
It is automatically called when an object is created (the programmer
does not call it directly).
●
If no constructor is declared, the program automatically provides a
default empty constructor.
⇒What is the access modifier for the default constructor?
⇒Can we create a private constructor? (Singleton Pattern)
17

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the role of constructors in encapsulation. A constructor is a special class function that shares the class name, lacks a return value, and is automatically invoked upon object creation. If no constructor is explicitly defined, the system provides a default empty one. It also raises key questions about access modifiers for constructors and the application of private constructors in design patterns like the Singleton Pattern.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What are the primary characteristics that distinguish a constructor from a regular class method?
2. Under what circumstances does a program automatically provide a default constructor?
3. When exactly is a constructor called, and who initiates this call?
4. Why might a programmer want to define a private constructor, and which design pattern is mentioned as an example?','e4eec8cc-2080-44c4-9727-54173f8b8998'::uuid,NULL,NULL,17,334,'2026-05-08 16:17:02.850172+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('cd3df55a-e1dc-4a16-9cf8-0977573b9656'::uuid,'5531c52e-2f10-49bd-ac07-9933aadc961a'::uuid,17,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Getters and Setters
18
•
Example:
public class Getset {
private int age;
// Getter for ''age''
public int getAge() {
return age;
}
// Setter for ''age'' with validation
public void setAge(int age) {
if (age >= 0) {
this.age = age;
} else {
System.out.println("Age cannot be negative.");
}
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
}
}
Getters and Setters
are methods used to
access and modify
the private fields of a
class, adhering to the
principles of
encapsulation

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the concept of Getters and Setters as a means to implement encapsulation in Java. By keeping class fields private, these methods provide a controlled way to access and modify data. The example demonstrates how a Setter can incorporate validation logic, such as ensuring an age is not negative, to maintain the integrity of an object''s internal state. This practice protects data from unauthorized or invalid changes while allowing external classes to interact with the object safely.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the primary purpose of using Getters and Setters in terms of encapsulation?
2. According to the code example, how does the ''setAge'' method handle an attempt to set a negative age?
3. Why is the ''age'' variable declared with the ''private'' access modifier?
4. How does providing a Setter method with validation improve the reliability of a class compared to using public fields?','24470749-7e6e-4125-b41e-99346246d012'::uuid,NULL,NULL,18,356,'2026-05-08 16:17:02.850234+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('a6d5704c-5b17-4a01-bf40-313ad9d043d4'::uuid,'5531c52e-2f10-49bd-ac07-9933aadc961a'::uuid,18,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Getters and Setters
19
•Encapsulation: Protects the internal state of an object.
•Control: Allows you to validate or modify data before it''s set.
•Consistency: Helps maintain consistent object state.
•Flexibility: Makes it easier to change the internal implementation
without affecting other code.
•Readability: Improves the readability and maintainability of the
code.
•Access Control: Allows creation of read-only or write-only
properties.
•Debugging: Enables easy tracking and debugging of changes to
the data.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment outlines the key benefits of using getters and setters in object-oriented programming. These methods are essential for encapsulation, as they protect an object''s internal state and allow for data validation and consistency. They provide flexibility for future code changes, improve readability, and enable precise access control, such as creating read-only properties. Additionally, they facilitate easier debugging by centralizing where data is accessed or modified.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. How do getters and setters help protect the internal state of an object?
2. In what way can setters be used to improve data validation and consistency?
3. How can getters and setters be used to create a read-only property?
4. Why does using these methods make it easier to change internal implementation without affecting external code?','6c45cd4d-22a9-4b07-a766-be5e11d91ebb'::uuid,NULL,NULL,19,350,'2026-05-08 16:17:02.850296+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('38e660fd-2f00-4328-9161-d4ae9d119788'::uuid,'5531c52e-2f10-49bd-ac07-9933aadc961a'::uuid,19,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
BEST PRACTICES
04
20

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces the fundamental best practices for using getters, setters, and encapsulation in Java. It highlights how these methods protect an object''s internal state and provide control over data through validation. Following these practices ensures code consistency, improves readability, and offers flexibility for future implementation changes. Additionally, it allows for fine-grained access control (read-only or write-only) and simplifies the debugging process by tracking how data is modified within an application.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What are the primary advantages of using encapsulation in Java classes?
2. How do setter methods help in maintaining a consistent object state through data validation?
3. In what way do getters and setters provide more flexibility than making class fields public?
4. How can a developer use access control to create a read-only property in a class?','8b30ed96-22a4-4c51-b7fd-ae8f1237c579'::uuid,NULL,NULL,20,243,'2026-05-08 16:17:02.850336+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('12631568-ae15-4bb9-b61a-32b5340e5178'::uuid,'5531c52e-2f10-49bd-ac07-9933aadc961a'::uuid,20,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Exercise 1 - Multiple constructor
Create a Car class with the following private attributes:
●
brand (string)
●
model (string)
●
year (int)
Add the following:
●
A default constructor that sets default values.
●
A parameterized constructor to initialize all attributes.
●
Setters and getters for all attributes.
Write a main function to:
●
Create an object using the default constructor and display its details.
●
Create another object using the parameterized constructor and display its
details.
21

=== SUMMARY ===
This exercise focuses on the practical application of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) principles by creating a Car class. It teaches how to implement encapsulation using private attributes and public getters/setters. Additionally, it demonstrates constructor overloading by requiring both a default constructor for preset values and a parameterized constructor for custom initialization. Learners will practice object instantiation and state management by testing these components within a main function to display object details.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. Why are the attributes brand, model, and year defined as private instead of public?
2. What is the difference between the default constructor and the parameterized constructor in this exercise?
3. What is the role of getters and setters in maintaining the encapsulation of the Car class?
4. How do you instantiate and display the details of an object using the parameterized constructor in the main function?','2ac64af1-92bb-489f-a3c6-96506c85e8fc'::uuid,NULL,NULL,21,377,'2026-05-08 16:17:02.8504+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('bbbd369b-47f9-4da3-972b-ce1d26dbe010'::uuid,'5531c52e-2f10-49bd-ac07-9933aadc961a'::uuid,21,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Exercise 2 - Constructor chaining
Create a Laptop class with attributes:
●
brand (string)
●
model (string)
●
price (double)
Add the following:
●
A default constructor.
●
A parameterized constructor to initialize all attributes.
●
Use constructor chaining to call one constructor from another.
Write a main function to test the class.
22

=== SUMMARY ===
This exercise focuses on the concept of constructor chaining within a Laptop class. It guides learners to define multiple constructors—a default one and a parameterized one—and demonstrates how to invoke one constructor from another to avoid code duplication. By implementing attributes like brand, model, and price, students learn to streamline object initialization and ensure consistent state management in object-oriented programming.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the primary benefit of using constructor chaining in a class like Laptop?
2. How do you call a parameterized constructor from a default constructor in C++ or Java syntax?
3. Which three specific attributes must be initialized within the Laptop class for this exercise?
4. What is the difference between a default constructor and a parameterized constructor in terms of how they handle object data?','aeebd6dd-31d3-4113-b179-200b3bb6bba6'::uuid,NULL,NULL,22,313,'2026-05-08 16:17:02.850484+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('4a950fa1-2ce9-4240-be59-4865215de024'::uuid,'5531c52e-2f10-49bd-ac07-9933aadc961a'::uuid,22,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Exercise 3 (Bonus)
Create a Library system using encapsulation and constructors.
1. Create a Book class with attributes: title, author, ISBN, price, and stock.
2. Create a Library class with:
a. An array of Book objects.
b. Methods to:
i.
Add new books.
ii. Search for books by title or author.
iii. Borrow books (reduce stock).
3. Write a main function to simulate library operations.
23

=== SUMMARY ===
This exercise challenges students to design a Library system by applying the principles of encapsulation and constructors. By creating both ''Book'' and ''Library'' classes, learners practice managing collections of objects and implementing business logic such as inventory control (stock reduction), data searching, and secure data handling. This practical application reinforces how classes interact within a larger system to simulate real-world operations while maintaining data integrity.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. How does the principle of encapsulation apply to the ''stock'' attribute of the Book class when a book is borrowed?
2. Why is it more efficient to use a Library class to manage multiple Book objects rather than handling them individually in the main function?
3. What specific logic should be included in the ''Borrow'' method to ensure data consistency when stock is updated?
4. How could constructors be used to ensure that every Book object added to the Library is initialized with valid data?','c83bfe67-6837-492f-ad9a-9e8fd48e2610'::uuid,NULL,NULL,23,360,'2026-05-08 16:17:02.850545+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('5a8354aa-6897-428a-981b-849caa84c9f7'::uuid,'5531c52e-2f10-49bd-ac07-9933aadc961a'::uuid,23,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
CONCLUSION
05
24

=== SUMMARY ===
This conclusion summarizes the core principles of Object-Oriented Programming covered in the lesson, specifically encapsulation, classes, and constructors. It defines encapsulation as the bundling of data and functions, distinguishes between classes and structs based on data security, and explains that constructors are automatic mechanisms for object creation that must be carefully designed to prevent errors. These concepts form the essential groundwork for building secure and organized software systems.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the main role of encapsulation in bundling data and functions within a class?
2. How do classes and structs differ regarding their default data security levels (private vs. public)?
3. Under what circumstances is a constructor executed in an OOP environment?
4. Why is proper constructor design essential to prevent errors during object instantiation?','da01346a-748c-4288-b892-d76e1aa6cf5f'::uuid,NULL,NULL,24,239,'2026-05-08 16:17:02.850585+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('5ebf72a8-3d31-43e0-9e11-7dfc516b5f00'::uuid,'5531c52e-2f10-49bd-ac07-9933aadc961a'::uuid,24,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Conclusion
1. Encapsulation is the foundation of OOP, focusing on bundling data and
functions within a class.
2. The main difference between a class and a struct lies in the level of data
security (private/public).
3. A constructor is a special mechanism in OOP that automatically executes
when an object is created and can cause errors if not properly designed.
25

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment provides a concluding overview of key Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) concepts. It defines encapsulation as the bundling of data and functions, identifies data security (private vs. public access) as the primary distinction between classes and structs, and explains the role of constructors as automatic initialization mechanisms. It also warns that poorly designed constructors can lead to errors during object creation.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is defined as the foundation of Object-Oriented Programming in this text?
2. What is the primary difference between a class and a struct regarding data security?
3. When does a constructor automatically execute in OOP?
4. Why is it important to properly design a constructor?','55662d3a-fa20-48c2-9669-9fe02b9764e2'::uuid,NULL,NULL,25,288,'2026-05-08 16:17:02.850636+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('af2e4359-c876-4162-a937-4476329b6e28'::uuid,'5531c52e-2f10-49bd-ac07-9933aadc961a'::uuid,25,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
EXTENSION:
CLASS DIAGRAM
06
26

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces supplemental or extended material concerning Class Diagrams, a core component of the Unified Modeling Language (UML). It focuses on the static structure of a system, detailing classes, attributes, operations, and the relationships between objects. The numbering suggests this is a specific module or series of slides (06-26) dedicated to deepening the learner''s understanding of structural modeling in software design.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the primary function of a Class Diagram in software development?
2. Which elements are typically included in a single class representation within a diagram?
3. How do Class Diagrams illustrate the relationships between different system components?
4. Why is a Class Diagram classified as a static structure diagram rather than a behavioral one?','b7214917-3587-4708-b18e-0d1d707a3a95'::uuid,NULL,NULL,26,224,'2026-05-08 16:17:02.850673+07');
