ĐẠI HỌC QUỐC GIA THÀNH PHỐ HỒ CHÍ MINH
TRƯỜNG ĐẠI HỌC BÁCH KHOA
Chapter 4: Abstract class & Interface
TP.HCM, 03/01/2025
Advanced Programming
CO2039

=== SUMMARY ===
Đoạn văn bản là trang tiêu đề giới thiệu Chương 4 của môn học Lập trình nâng cao (CO2039) tại Trường Đại học Bách khoa, Đại học Quốc gia TP.HCM. Nội dung chính của chương này tập trung vào hai khái niệm quan trọng trong lập trình hướng đối tượng là Lớp trừu tượng (Abstract class) và Giao diện (Interface), nhằm giúp sinh viên hiểu rõ cách thiết kế và tổ chức mã nguồn một cách linh hoạt.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. Chương 4 của môn học Lập trình nâng cao tập trung vào những kiến thức trọng tâm nào?
2. Tài liệu này thuộc về cơ sở đào tạo nào và được cập nhật vào thời gian nào?
3. Mã học phần được nhắc đến trong đoạn văn bản là gì?
4. Tên tiếng Anh của môn học được đề cập trong tài liệu là gì?','ea6eec37-76e8-432a-a554-f59d5a6def96'::uuid,NULL,NULL,1,222,'2026-05-08 16:24:34.239039+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('7cd633f4-6794-4f0d-b5be-21426fea5e1d'::uuid,'6a708d03-6676-4eee-a3f7-a1680293e500'::uuid,3,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
What is Abstract class?
An abstract class is a class that cannot be instantiated. It serves as a blueprint
for other classes.
Key characteristics:
●
Can include abstract methods (methods without implementation).
●
May also include concrete methods (methods with implementation).
●
Used when classes share common behavior but also require customization
in subclasses.
●
Supports inheritance.
4

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains that an abstract class is a non-instantiable class that acts as a blueprint for others. It can contain both abstract methods (without code) and concrete methods (with code). Abstract classes are primarily used to share common behaviors through inheritance while allowing subclasses to implement specific details, ensuring a structured approach to defining related objects.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What does it mean for an abstract class to be incapable of instantiation?
2. What is the difference between an abstract method and a concrete method within an abstract class?
3. In what scenario is it beneficial to use an abstract class instead of a standard class?
4. How does an abstract class support the concept of inheritance in object-oriented programming?','3ea6a378-d048-40d9-8736-ce9eaf782fa8'::uuid,NULL,NULL,4,305,'2026-05-08 16:24:34.239179+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('f47ecae0-d7fb-4d93-a790-4c17e37eb5f6'::uuid,'6a708d03-6676-4eee-a3f7-a1680293e500'::uuid,4,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Example in Java
abstract class Shape {
abstract void draw(); // Abstract method
void info() {         // Concrete method
System.out.println("This is a shape");
}
}
class Circle extends Shape {
void draw() {
System.out.println("Drawing Circle");
}
}
5

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment provides a practical Java example of an abstract class and inheritance. It demonstrates the ''Shape'' abstract class, which includes both an abstract method (''draw'') requiring subclass implementation and a concrete method (''info'') with a predefined body. The ''Circle'' subclass illustrates how to extend an abstract class by providing the specific implementation for the abstract ''draw'' method. This example highlights the role of abstract classes as blueprints that enforce certain behaviors while allowing shared functionality.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the main difference between the draw() and info() methods within the Shape class?
2. Why is the Circle class required to provide an implementation for the draw() method?
3. Based on this example, what keyword is used to define a class that cannot be instantiated and may contain abstract methods?
4. How does the Circle class indicate that it is a subclass of the Shape class in Java?','9cf4d44e-2331-4c37-a17e-0c13f0051f0f'::uuid,NULL,NULL,5,313,'2026-05-08 16:24:34.239222+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('b3e6a542-0ffa-4a43-bb2e-26480a0c749f'::uuid,'6a708d03-6676-4eee-a3f7-a1680293e500'::uuid,5,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
What is Interface?
An interface is a classlike construct that contains only constants and abstract
methods.
Key characteristics:
●
Cannot be instantiated (only classes that implements interfaces can be
instantiated).
●
Why not just use Abstract class? ⇒Java does not permit multiple
inheritance but allow a class can implement multiple interfaces.
Can an interface be implemented by multiple classes?
●
No instance variables; only constants allowed.
6

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces the concept of an Interface in Java, defining it as a construct limited to constants and abstract methods. Key features include its inability to be instantiated and its role in enabling multiple inheritance, which is otherwise restricted in Java classes. Unlike abstract classes, interfaces do not allow instance variables, only constants. They serve as a mechanism for multiple classes to implement shared behaviors, providing flexibility in software design.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What are the only two types of components allowed within an interface?
2. How does an interface help overcome Java''s restriction on multiple inheritance?
3. Can you create an object directly from an interface? Why or why not?
4. What is the difference between an interface and a class regarding instance variables?','06f091ce-a1c0-4e8f-8c2d-2158ff459072'::uuid,NULL,NULL,6,330,'2026-05-08 16:24:34.239276+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('1950a42e-7aae-4479-8a67-d75d1e292186'::uuid,'6a708d03-6676-4eee-a3f7-a1680293e500'::uuid,6,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Creating an Interface
File InterfaceName.java:
modifier interface InterfaceName {
constants declarations;
methods signatures;
}
File ClassName.java:
modifier Class ClassName implements InterfaceName {
methods implementation;
}
7
⇒modifier is public or not used.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the fundamental syntax for defining and implementing interfaces in Java. It details the structure of an interface file, which contains constants and method signatures, and illustrates how a class uses the ''implements'' keyword to provide concrete method implementations. Additionally, it specifies that the access modifiers for these components are restricted to ''public'' or the default (no modifier).

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What are the two types of declarations typically found inside a Java interface?
2. Which keyword is required for a class to use the methods defined in an interface?
3. According to the text, what are the two possible options for the ''modifier'' in an interface declaration?
4. In which file type is the actual implementation of the interface methods written?','f6edd80d-6907-4f25-9fbb-b0d04e76df23'::uuid,NULL,NULL,7,278,'2026-05-08 16:24:34.23932+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('d18b6ffd-494e-4581-99d7-f90ed94ecb71'::uuid,'6a708d03-6676-4eee-a3f7-a1680293e500'::uuid,7,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Hands-on exercise
interface Drawable {
void draw();  // Abstract method
}
class Rectangle implements Drawable {
public void draw() {
System.out.println("Drawing Rectangle");
}
}
8
Add new interface Area to calculate the area of a shape and implement it in
Rectangle. Write main function to test.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment provides a practical exercise to reinforce Java interface concepts. It demonstrates how to define an interface with an abstract method and how a class implements that interface. The exercise challenges learners to implement multiple interfaces by creating a new ''Area'' interface and applying it to the existing ''Rectangle'' class. This task highlights Java''s ability to support multiple interface implementation, allowing classes to adhere to multiple contracts and promoting a decoupled, flexible design architecture.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. Which keyword is used in the example to connect the Rectangle class to the Drawable interface?
2. What are the requirements for the Rectangle class once it declares that it implements the Drawable interface?
3. How does this exercise demonstrate Java''s ability to handle multiple inheritance of type through interfaces?
4. In the ''Area'' interface mentioned in the task, what would be an appropriate method signature for calculating the area?','3ac3c268-a419-4bf4-8176-47224aa9e73d'::uuid,NULL,NULL,8,334,'2026-05-08 16:24:34.239368+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('536e657d-7195-47ca-ab83-d756003d35a5'::uuid,'6a708d03-6676-4eee-a3f7-a1680293e500'::uuid,8,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
INTERFACE IN
DEPTH
02
9

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces a deeper exploration of Java interfaces, moving beyond basic syntax to architectural benefits. It focuses on how interfaces solve compile-time dependency issues and facilitate dynamic method resolution. By decoupling method definitions from the class hierarchy, interfaces allow unrelated classes to share behaviors, promote composition over inheritance, and increase code flexibility. This section highlights the transition from simple implementation to advanced software design patterns using interfaces.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. How do interfaces help resolve the ''Compile-Time Dependency Problem'' in software architecture?
2. What is the significance of ''Dynamic Method Resolution'' when using interface references?
3. How do interfaces allow unrelated classes to share functionality without using a rigid inheritance tree?
4. Why is ''composition over inheritance'' considered a key benefit of using interfaces in complex systems?','c50d08c6-9eeb-4757-ac0b-fc026d2f94d1'::uuid,NULL,NULL,9,256,'2026-05-08 16:24:34.239406+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('e8a86119-c748-4a19-a0cd-5c26bb77288c'::uuid,'6a708d03-6676-4eee-a3f7-a1680293e500'::uuid,9,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
More in Interfaces (1)
Compile-Time Dependency Problem:
●
Normally, methods from one class to another need compile-time presence for
signature checks.
●
This creates rigid inheritance hierarchies, pushing shared functionality higher in the
class tree.
Interfaces to the Rescue:
●
Allow dynamic method resolution at runtime.
●
Decouple method definitions from the class inheritance hierarchy.
●
Enable unrelated classes to implement the same interface.
Key Benefit: Promote composition over inheritance and avoid the rigidity of deep class
hierarchies.
10

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains how interfaces solve the issues caused by rigid compile-time dependencies in standard inheritance. By allowing dynamic method resolution at runtime, interfaces decouple method definitions from the class tree. This enables unrelated classes to share the same behavior, providing flexibility and promoting composition over inheritance to avoid the complexity of deep, rigid class hierarchies.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. How do compile-time dependencies affect the structure of inheritance hierarchies?
2. In what way do interfaces facilitate dynamic method resolution at runtime?
3. Why is it beneficial for unrelated classes to be able to implement the same interface?
4. What is the significance of promoting composition over inheritance in software design?','7dd7be3f-41b6-426b-8437-3f6c9eb79d49'::uuid,NULL,NULL,10,344,'2026-05-08 16:24:34.239461+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('1d05b7d4-3aac-481c-b763-a9aad0cd9f2e'::uuid,'6a708d03-6676-4eee-a3f7-a1680293e500'::uuid,10,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
More in Interfaces (2)
Dynamic Method Resolution:
●
The actual method is determined at runtime, not compile time.
●
Calling code interacts with an interface or abstract class reference,
not specific implementations.
●
Benefit:
○
Decouples calling code from concrete classes.
○
Allows flexibility to add/change implementations without
modifying the calling code.
11

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains Dynamic Method Resolution, where the specific method to be executed is determined at runtime rather than compile time. By utilizing interface or abstract class references, the calling code is decoupled from concrete implementations. This architectural approach offers the flexibility to modify or add new implementations without changing the existing calling code, which enhances system maintainability.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. At what point is the actual method implementation determined during dynamic method resolution?
2. Instead of concrete implementations, what type of references does the calling code interact with?
3. How does dynamic method resolution impact the coupling between calling code and concrete classes?
4. What is the primary benefit of being able to add or change implementations without modifying the calling code?','25488e5d-96d0-47ca-b7c1-7fe75f34f4f0'::uuid,NULL,NULL,11,318,'2026-05-08 16:24:34.239513+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('f29f6574-2dbc-411c-83c9-565bf555c659'::uuid,'6a708d03-6676-4eee-a3f7-a1680293e500'::uuid,11,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
More in Interfaces - Example
interface Shape { void draw(); }
class Circle implements Shape {
public void draw() { System.out.println("Drawing a Circle"); }
}
class Square implements Shape {
public void draw() { System.out.println("Drawing a Square"); }
}
public class Main {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Shape shape = new Circle();  // Interface reference, dynamic resolution
shape.draw();  // Calls Circle''s draw() method
shape = new Square();  // Switch to a different implementation
shape.draw();  // Calls Square''s draw() method
}
}
12

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment provides a code example demonstrating how interfaces enable dynamic method resolution and polymorphism. By defining a common ''Shape'' interface, different classes like ''Circle'' and ''Square'' can provide their own implementations of the ''draw'' method. The ''Main'' class shows that a single interface reference can point to different concrete objects at runtime. This illustrates how interfaces decouple calling code from specific implementations, allowing the program to determine which method to execute dynamically based on the actual object being referenced.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What allows the ''shape'' variable to hold both a Circle and a Square object in this example?
2. Explain the concept of dynamic resolution as it applies to the ''shape.draw()'' calls.
3. Why is it advantageous to use the ''Shape'' interface reference instead of specific ''Circle'' or ''Square'' references in the Main class?
4. What must a class do if it implements the ''Shape'' interface but does not want to be abstract?','5ac9166e-8cb9-456b-8d1f-842e6c7764c7'::uuid,NULL,NULL,12,401,'2026-05-08 16:24:34.239568+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('5242ea81-95e4-4bea-bbf7-10d380682395'::uuid,'6a708d03-6676-4eee-a3f7-a1680293e500'::uuid,12,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Does interface solve diamond problem?
Interfaces help address the diamond problem in multiple inheritance, but indirectly:
●
The diamond problem arises when a class inherits from two parent classes that
define methods with the same name and signature, leading to ambiguity.
●
In Java:
○
Classes do not support multiple inheritance, which avoids this issue.
○
Interfaces support multiple inheritance, but there’s no ambiguity because a
class implementing multiple interfaces must explicitly define the methods it
inherits.
○
If a default method in two interfaces clashes, the implementing class must
override it to resolve the conflict explicitly.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains how Java addresses the diamond problem using interfaces. While Java classes avoid ambiguity by prohibiting multiple inheritance, interfaces allow it. Ambiguity is prevented because Java requires the implementing class to explicitly define or override clashing methods, particularly when multiple interfaces provide conflicting default methods. This mechanism ensures that the developer explicitly resolves which implementation should be used, thereby eliminating the functional ambiguity typical of the diamond problem.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the ''diamond problem'' and why does it lead to ambiguity in programming?
2. How does Java''s approach to class inheritance differ from its approach to interface inheritance regarding the diamond problem?
3. Why is there no ambiguity when a class inherits methods with the same signature from multiple interfaces in Java?
4. In the case of clashing default methods from two different interfaces, what action must the implementing class take?','3e323399-9d33-40ca-9fe2-b4b1fd1de85e'::uuid,NULL,NULL,13,426,'2026-05-08 16:24:34.239636+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('e8c89d3a-fdc0-44dc-865a-0178c44271df'::uuid,'6a708d03-6676-4eee-a3f7-a1680293e500'::uuid,13,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Example: Interface in diamond problem
interface A {
default void show() { System.out.println("A"); }
}
interface B {
default void show() { System.out.println("B"); }
}
class C implements A, B {
// Resolving the diamond problem
@Override
public void show() {
A.super.show(); // Explicitly choose which interface method to
call
}
}
14

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment illustrates how Java handles the diamond problem when a class implements multiple interfaces containing conflicting default methods. To resolve the ambiguity, the implementing class must override the method. The example shows how to use the ''InterfaceName.super.methodName()'' syntax to explicitly choose a specific interface''s implementation, ensuring the compiler knows exactly which version of the method to execute.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. Why must class C override the show() method when implementing both interface A and B?
2. What is the specific syntax used to call a default method from a particular parent interface?
3. What would happen if the ''show()'' method in class C did not use ''A.super.show()'' or ''B.super.show()''?
4. How does the use of default methods in interfaces differ from standard abstract methods regarding the diamond problem?','71df6e8e-ba6c-4ab2-9061-8d78941af353'::uuid,NULL,NULL,14,311,'2026-05-08 16:24:34.239715+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('cee7de1e-331a-4a3d-a552-284c3dc9f15c'::uuid,'6a708d03-6676-4eee-a3f7-a1680293e500'::uuid,14,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
CONCLUSION
03
15

=== SUMMARY ===
This section concludes the discussion on how Java interfaces resolve the diamond problem. By disallowing multiple inheritance in classes but allowing it in interfaces, Java avoids ambiguity. When method conflicts occur due to multiple interface implementation, the developer must explicitly override the clashing method. This ensures that the implementing class provides a clear, unambiguous behavior, often by selecting a specific parent implementation using the ''InterfaceName.super.methodName()'' syntax, effectively solving the inheritance complexities found in other languages.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. How does the requirement to override clashing default methods help resolve the diamond problem in Java?
2. Why do interfaces in Java allow multiple inheritance while classes are restricted to single inheritance?
3. In the provided example, what syntax is used to explicitly call a specific interface''s default method implementation?
4. What occurs if a class implements two interfaces with identical method signatures but neither interface provides a default implementation?','6c58bcb1-a342-449c-9d9a-745c7d4148ec'::uuid,NULL,NULL,15,286,'2026-05-08 16:24:34.239758+07');
