=== SUMMARY ===
This segment presents a detailed comparison between abstract classes and interfaces in Java across five key features. While neither can be instantiated directly, they differ significantly in their capabilities: abstract classes support concrete methods and instance variables with any modifier, but are limited to single inheritance. In contrast, interfaces allow multiple inheritance and only contain constants and abstract methods (which are implicitly public). This comparison serves as a guide for choosing the correct tool for defining object behavior and structure.

