INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('797613af-1855-4598-822f-870122a0292a'::uuid,'6a708d03-6676-4eee-a3f7-a1680293e500'::uuid,19,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
The Cloneable interface
20
public class Circle extends Shape implements Cloneable {
private int x, y, radius;
// Constructor
...
// Clone method
@Override
public Object clone() {
try { return super.clone(); } // Shallow copy
catch (CloneNotSupportedException ex) { return null; }
}
}
Main function:
Circle c1 = new Circle(1, 2, 3); // Create an instance of Circle
Circle c2 = (Circle) c1.clone(); // Clone c1 into c2
System.out.println("Cloned Circle: " + c2); // Verify cloning

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment illustrates how to implement the Cloneable interface in Java. It demonstrates overriding the clone() method to perform a shallow copy using super.clone() and handling the CloneNotSupportedException. The example shows the creation of a Circle object and how to properly use the clone method, including the necessary type casting, to create a duplicate instance.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
