=== SUMMARY ===
This segment outlines advanced UML concepts, focusing on Sequence and Activity Diagrams. It detail the mechanics of system interactions through messages, combined fragments (loops, branches), and constraints. It also explores Activity Diagrams, including control/object flows and exception handling. The document bridges theory and practice with case studies—like a coffee machine and a submission system—and introduces model organization through packages and UML metamodels. Overall, it provides a structured path from individual behavioral diagrams to integrated system modeling and advanced extension mechanisms.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What are ''Combined Fragments'' in a Sequence Diagram, and how are they used to model logic like loops or concurrency?
2. In the context of Activity Diagrams, what is the distinction between ''Control Flows'' and ''Object Flows''?
3. Which practical case studies are provided in Chapter 8 to demonstrate how different UML diagrams work together?
4. How does the UML allow for the organization and structuring of large models according to Chapter 9?','4bd020a1-91ca-4891-932a-8e0684ac2f9b'::uuid,NULL,NULL,12,1114,'2026-05-08 16:23:24.129061+07');
