Figure 2.1 shows a taxonomy of the 14 UML diagrams [35], giv-
ing a very rough categorization. As the ﬁgure shows, we differentiate
between structure diagrams and behavior diagrams. The behavior di-
agrams include the interaction diagrams, which in turn consist of four
diagrams (see Chapter 6).
A diagram is usually enclosed by a rectangle with a pentagon in the
Notation for diagram
frame
top left-hand corner. This pentagon contains the diagram type and the
name of the diagram. Optionally, parameters may be speciﬁed following
the name which then can be used within the diagram. Figure 2.2 con-

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces UML diagrams as graphical representations of system models. In version 2.4.1, UML provides 14 distinct diagrams categorized into two main types: structure diagrams and behavior diagrams (which include interaction diagrams). The text explains that diagrams are typically presented within a specific frame—a rectangle with a pentagon in the top left corner. This header contains the diagram''s type and name, and can include optional parameters to help specify the system''s architecture or dynamic processes effectively.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. How many different types of diagrams are available in UML version 2.4.1?
2. Into which two primary categories does the UML taxonomy divide its 14 diagrams?
3. Which sub-category of diagrams falls under ''Behavior Diagrams'' and consists of four specific types?
4. According to the notation for a diagram frame, what information must be included in the pentagon at the top left corner?','eb22ba92-982d-40a0-a188-5990e067c766'::uuid,NULL,NULL,27,622,'2026-05-08 16:23:24.131731+07');
