●
foo(int i) and foo(int i, int j) are different
●
foo(int i) and foo(int k) are the same
●
foo(int i, double d) and foo(double d, int i) are
different

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces the concept of method differentiation in the context of polymorphism, specifically focusing on method overloading. It explains that for methods to be unique, they must have different names or variations in the number and types of their parameters. The examples highlight that changing parameter names does not create a new method, whereas changing the quantity or the specific sequence of parameter types results in distinct method signatures.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What are the three specific ways methods can differ to be considered distinct according to this text?
2. Why does the text state that ''foo(int i)'' and ''foo(int k)'' are the same?
3. How does changing the order of parameter types (e.g., int then double vs. double then int) affect method definition?
4. Based on the rules provided, would adding a third parameter to a method create a ''different'' method signature?','8dfd4596-02b5-4496-88a7-12382e2b17bc'::uuid,NULL,NULL,5,306,'2026-05-08 16:24:56.701845+07');
