Figure 3.2
Representation of system
boundaries
3.2 Actors
To describe a system completely, it is essential to document not only
what the system can do but also who actually works and interacts with
the system. In the use case diagram, actors always interact with the sys-
Actor
X
tem in the context of their use cases, that is, the use cases with which
they are associated. The example in Figure 3.2 contains only the actor
Professor, who can query student data, announce exams, and issue cer-
tiﬁcates. Actors are represented by stick ﬁgures, rectangles (containing
the additional information «actor»), or by a freely deﬁnable symbol. The
notation alternatives are shown in Figure 3.3. These three notation al-
ternatives are all equally valid. As we can see from this example, actors
can be human (e.g., student or professor) or non-human (e.g., e-mail
server). The symbols used to represent the actors in a speciﬁc use case
diagram depend on the person creating the model or the tool used. Note
in particular that non-human actors can also be portrayed as stick ﬁg-
ures, even if this seems counterintuitive.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the visual representation of system boundaries and actors in use case diagrams. Use cases are contained within a rectangle symbolizing the system boundary. Actors, which represent entities interacting with the system, can be human or non-human. They are depicted using stick figures, rectangles with the «actor» stereotype, or custom symbols. Identifying actors is essential for a complete system description, as they define who interacts with the system''s functionalities in the context of specific use cases.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What graphical element is used to represent the boundaries of a system in a use case diagram?
2. What are the three notation alternatives for representing an actor, and are they all equally valid?
3. Can a non-human entity, such as an e-mail server, be represented as a stick figure in a diagram?
4. Why is it important to document actors when describing a software system''s functionality?','d3304282-c3f0-4df4-835a-54733b9d742a'::uuid,NULL,NULL,36,664,'2026-05-08 16:23:24.13281+07');
