An actor is always clearly outside the system, i.e., a user is never part
of the system and is therefore never implemented. Data about the user,
however, can be available within the system and can be represented, for
example, by a class in a class diagram (see Chapter 4). Sometimes it is
difﬁcult to decide whether an element is part of the system to be imple-

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment defines the role of actors in use case diagrams, emphasizing that they exist outside the system boundary. Actors are categorized as human or non-human, active (initiators) or passive (providers), and primary (beneficiaries) or secondary (support). Despite these functional differences, UML uses identical graphical notations for all types. The text highlights that while systems may store data about actors, the actors themselves are never part of the implemented system, maintaining a clear distinction between the system and its external users.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What are the primary differences between an active actor and a passive actor?
2. How does the benefit received from a use case distinguish a primary actor from a secondary actor?
3. Are there specific graphical symbols used to distinguish between human and non-human actors in a use case diagram?
4. Why is an actor always considered to be outside the system boundary, even if the system stores data about them?','6a419ccf-53be-4adc-b81b-4aa2664f7db9'::uuid,NULL,NULL,37,768,'2026-05-08 16:23:24.132952+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('b0ebc7e3-30f1-498f-a53c-ccccc9486701'::uuid,'f0f814ac-a731-4f96-83e3-c1a59ee880d5'::uuid,36,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
3.3 Associations
27
In the examples in Figure 3.4, we connected the actors with use cases via
solid lines without explaining this in more detail. An actor is connected
with the use cases via associations which express that the actor com-
municates with the system and uses a certain functionality. Every actor
must communicate with at least one use case. Otherwise, we would have
an actor that does not interact with the system. In the same way, every
use case must be in a relationship with at least one actor. If this were
not the case, we would have modeled a functionality that is not used by
anyone and is therefore irrelevant.
An association is always binary, meaning that it is always speciﬁed
between one use case and one actor. Multiplicities may be speciﬁed
for the association ends. If a multiplicity greater than 1 is speciﬁed for
the actor’s association end, this means that more than one instance of
an actor is involved in the execution of the use case. If we look at the
example in Figure 3.5, one to three students and precisely one assistant
is involved in the execution of the use case Conduct oral exam. If no
multiplicity is speciﬁed for the actor’s association end, 1 is assumed as
the default value. The multiplicity at the use case’s association end is
mostly unrestricted and is therefore only rarely speciﬁed explicitly.
1..3
Laboratory Assignment
Conduct
oral exam
Assistant
Student
Figure 3.5
Multiplicities in
associations

=== SUMMARY ===
This section explains associations in use case diagrams, which are solid lines representing the communication between actors and use cases. It emphasizes that every actor must be linked to at least one use case and every use case to at least one actor to ensure the model''s relevance. Associations are binary, connecting one actor to one use case. Multiplicities indicate the number of actor instances involved; if unspecified for an actor, the default is one. This helps define exactly who participates in specific system functionalities.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What does an association line between an actor and a use case represent in a UML diagram?
2. Why is it considered a modeling error if a use case is not connected to any actor?
