Error 6: Modeling redundant use cases
When modeling use cases, it is very tempting to create a separate use
case for each possible situation. For example, in the use case diagram in
Figure 3.17(a), we have modeled separate use cases for creating, updat-
ing, and deleting courses. This shows the different options available for
editing a course in the system. In such a small use case diagram as that
shown in Figure 3.17(a), it is not a problem to show the differentiations
at such a detailed level.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment identifies two pitfalls in use case modeling: functional decomposition and redundant use cases. Functional decomposition is the error of breaking a use case into sub-functions (like ''Log in'' or ''Enter data'') that cannot stand alone; these should instead be part of the use case description. Redundancy occurs when separate use cases are created for every variation of an action, such as creating or deleting. While acceptable in small diagrams, these should be grouped into broader use cases to ensure the diagram remains manageable.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
