Objects represent the concrete forms of classes and are referred to as
Instance
their instances. The relevant characteristics of the instances of a class
Characteristics of
classes ...
are described through the deﬁnition of structural characteristics (at-
tributes) and behavior (operations). Operations enable objects to com-
municate with one another and to act and react.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the relationship between objects and classes in UML. An object diagram provides a snapshot of a system at a specific time, where object values may change. To manage complexity, classes serve as blueprints or construction plans for groups of objects with identical characteristics. A class defines structural attributes and behavioral operations for its instances. Objects are the concrete forms of these classes. By using classes, developers can describe common behaviors and structures without modeling every individual object separately, facilitating maintenance and clarity.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What does it mean when an object is not represented in a specific object diagram snapshot?
2. Why is it impractical to model every individual person or object in a complex system separately?
3. How is the relationship between a class and an instance defined in object-oriented modeling?
4. What are the two main types of characteristics used to describe the instances of a class?','8bad5849-99fb-4237-8abf-5cafc0b9ece5'::uuid,NULL,NULL,62,817,'2026-05-08 16:23:24.136481+07');
