4.2.5 Visibility Markers
The visibility of attributes and operations speciﬁes who is and who is not
permitted to access them. If an attribute or operation does not have a vis-
ibility speciﬁed, no default visibility is assumed. Table 4.1 lists the types
of visibilities and their meaning in UML. Only an object itself knows
the values of attributes that are marked as private. In contrast, anyone
can view attributes marked as public. Access to protected attributes is
reserved for the class itself and its subclasses. If a class has a package
attribute, only classes that are in the same package as this class may ac-
cess this attribute. Accordingly, the visibility of an operation speciﬁes
who is permitted to use the functionality of the operation. Examples are
given in Figure 4.4(c) on page 53. Note that the meaning of visibilities
can vary in different programming and modeling languages even if they
have the same name in the different languages.
Visibilities are used to realize information hiding, an important con-
cept in computing. Marking the attributes that represent the state of an
object as private protects this state against unauthorized access. Access
is therefore only possible via a clearly deﬁned interface, such as via
operations that are declared public.
In some cases, class diagrams contain only those attributes and op-
erations that are visible externally. Attributes and operations of classes

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains UML parameter specifications and visibility markers. It describes how parameter directions (in, out, inout) manage data flow and how visibility levels—public (+), private (-), protected (#), and package (~)—restrict access to class members. A central theme is ''information hiding,'' where private attributes protect an object''s internal state, requiring external interaction to occur through a public interface. The text also notes that private members may be omitted from diagrams depending on the intended implementation detail.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What are the three parameter directions in UML, and how is the ''out'' direction used to handle return values?
2. How do public, private, protected, and package visibilities differ in terms of who can access a class attribute?
3. What is the concept of ''information hiding,'' and how do visibility markers help implement it?
4. Why might a designer choose to exclude private attributes or operations from a class diagram?','963802a2-c75e-4671-9e3b-2edfcaf3fd83'::uuid,NULL,NULL,68,732,'2026-05-08 16:23:24.137325+07');
