the visible characteristics of the students who attend the professor’s lec-
ture because the professor does not know them.
A non-navigable association end is indicated by the explicit speciﬁ-
cation of an X at the association end concerned. For example, if such
an X appears at the association end of A for an association between the
classes A and B, this means that B cannot access the attributes and op-
erations of A—not even the public ones. Bidirectional edges without
arrowheads or X at their ends do not provide any information about the
navigation direction but in practice, bidirectional navigability is usually
assumed. The navigation direction represents a hint for the subsequent
implementation because in object-oriented programming languages, as-
sociations are realized as references to the associated objects. An associ-
ation can also be represented in this way in the class diagram, that is, as
an attribute with the appropriate multiplicity, whereby the type of the at-
tribute is the class of the corresponding partner objects. This representa-
tion has the same semantics as a navigable association end. Figure 4.12

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains binary associations in UML, distinguishing between reading direction and navigability. Reading direction, indicated by a small triangle, guides the verbal interpretation of the relationship. Navigability, shown via arrowheads or an ''X'' for non-navigability, dictates whether an object can access the attributes and operations of its partner. The text emphasizes that navigability and reading direction are independent concepts and notes that associations are typically implemented as object references or attributes in programming languages.

