In the example in Figure 4.28, the class Person is abstract. Hence,
there cannot be any instances of Person itself but there can be instances
of the speciﬁc subclasses Employee and Student.
Similarly to the abstract class, an interface also does not have an im-
Interface
plementation or any direct instances. An interface represents a contract.
The classes that enter into this contract, that is, the classes that imple-
ment the interface, obligate themselves to provide the behavior speciﬁed
by the interface. In contrast to the relationship between an abstract class
and its subclasses, an “is a” relationship between an interface and the
classes that implement it is not necessary. Operations of interfaces never
have an implementation.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment defines abstract classes and interfaces in UML. Abstract classes cannot be instantiated and are used to capture common characteristics for subclasses. They may contain abstract operations, which lack implementation and must be defined in concrete subclasses. Interfaces serve as contracts, obliging implementing classes to provide specific behaviors without requiring a strict ''is a'' inheritance relationship. Unlike abstract classes, interface operations never include implementations. The text also introduces generalization sets for multiple classification, allowing objects to be categorized by independent criteria like job type or financing.

