=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the distinction between classes and data types in UML. Unlike objects, data type values lack unique identities; identical values are indistinguishable. UML categorizes data types into three forms: general data types (using «datatype»), which can have internal attributes; primitive data types (using «primitive» or pre-defined types like String and Integer), which lack internal structure; and enumerations (using «enumeration»), which represent a fixed list of values called literals. All are visualized similarly to classes but distinguished by specific keywords.

