#include <iostream>
class A
{
public:
int n;
int f() {n++; return n;}
int f2() {f(); return n;}
};
class B: public A
{
public:
int f(){n--; return n;}
};
int main()
{
A a; B b;
a.n = 1; b.n = 2;
a.f2(); b.f2();
std::cout<<a.n <<" " << b.n;
return 0;
}

=== SUMMARY ===
This C++ code segment illustrates inheritance and static binding. It defines a base class A and a derived class B. Although class B redefines method f(), the method f2() (inherited from A) calls the base class version of f() because f() is not declared as virtual. This demonstrates that without polymorphism/virtual functions, the compiler resolves function calls at compile-time based on the class where the calling method is defined, rather than the actual object type at runtime.

