5.7 Summary
A state machine diagram can be used to show the states in which a sys-
tem or an object can ﬁnd itself during its “life cycle”, that is, from its
creation to its destruction. The diagram also shows the conditions under
which the transitions between these states occur. Events and activities
triggered by these events can be modeled in the diagram. You can also
specify guards that must apply for an event to trigger related activi-
ties or a state transition. Additional concepts allow you to model more
complex state machine diagrams. Parallelization and synchronization
nodes, as well as orthogonal states, enable you to model simultaneously
active states and chains of states. The shallow and deep history states, as
well as entry and exit points, allow a deﬁned entry into transitive nested
substates of composite states. The most important elements of the state
machine diagram are summarized in Table 5.2.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains that state machine diagrams model an object''s life cycle, detailing states, transitions, events, and activities. It highlights the role of guards in conditional transitions and introduces advanced concepts for managing complexity. These include orthogonal states and synchronization nodes for parallel behaviors, as well as history states and entry/exit points for navigating nested composite states. Essentially, the diagram provides a framework to visualize how objects change state and assign variable values in response to specific events throughout their existence.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What specific period of an object''s existence does a state machine diagram aim to model?
2. What is the function of a ''guard'' within a state machine diagram?
3. Which concepts enable the representation of multiple simultaneously active states or chains of states?
4. How do history states and entry/exit points facilitate the modeling of composite states?','2327da7b-e137-430f-99f8-ea1d6357aec5'::uuid,NULL,NULL,115,542,'2026-05-08 16:23:24.143272+07');
