This segment introduces ''filters and assertions'' in UML sequence diagrams, which manage valid, invalid, and unspecified traces. It explains the ''ignore'' fragment, used to mark messages that occur but are irrelevant to the modeled functionality, such as background status updates. In contrast, the ''consider'' fragment explicitly lists mandatory relevant messages; any message not included in this list is automatically treated as irrelevant. These fragments help modelers focus on specific system behaviors while acknowledging the complexity of real-world interactions and communication protocols.

