6.7 The Communication, Timing, and Interaction
Overview Diagrams
In addition to the sequence diagram, UML supports three further types
of interaction diagrams:
• Communication diagram
• Timing diagram
• Interaction overview diagram
The four types of interaction diagrams of UML are generally equiv-
alent for simple interactions as they are based on the same basic ele-
ments. With the speciﬁcation of the communication partners involved
and the messages exchanged, they all describe certain communication
situations. However, the focus is different for each type of diagram.
In this section, we brieﬂy compare the four interaction diagrams in
examples. The examples are illustrated in Figures 6.33 to 6.36, show-
ing various aspects of the communication between a student and the
e-learning system of a university.
Figure 6.33 shows the log-in process as a sequence diagram. There
are three interaction partners: student, e-learning system, and database.
The student wants to log in to the system and therefore sends a cor-
responding message to the e-learning system. A query to the database
veriﬁes the access rights and the student receives a positive response

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment illustrates the Transfer Object Assembler pattern via a sequence diagram and introduces UML''s four interaction diagram types: Sequence, Communication, Timing, and Interaction Overview diagrams. While these diagrams share common elements and are equivalent for basic interactions, each emphasizes different aspects of communication. The text begins a comparative analysis using a university e-learning system login scenario involving a student, the system, and a database to demonstrate how these diagrams represent interaction partners and message exchanges.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the purpose of the Transfer Object Assembler (TOA) pattern as shown in the sequence diagram?
