To send signals, you can use send signal actions. Send signal actions
Send signal action
S
are denoted with a “convex pentagon”—a rectangle with a tip that pro-
trudes to the right. The action Send grade in Figure 7.3(b) is an example
of a send signal action.
7.2.2 Call Behavior Actions
Actions can call activities themselves. These actions are referred to as
Call behavior action
A
call behavior actions and are marked with an inverted fork symbol. This
fork symbol indicates a hierarchy. It symbolizes that the execution of
this action starts another activity, thus dividing the system into various
parts. Figure 7.3(a) shows an example of a call behavior action. In this
diagram, the action Issue certiﬁcate in the activity Organize exam refers
to an activity that speciﬁes Issue certiﬁcate in more detail. Within the
context of the activity Organize exam, the internal steps that lead to the
issue of a certiﬁcate are not relevant. Therefore, Issue certiﬁcate is seen
as an atomic unit here, even though it involves a process with multiple
actions.
The content of the called activity can be depicted elsewhere in this or
even another activity diagram in the form of an activity with the usual
notation for activities that we have already seen. Figure 7.3(b) shows
the details of the called activity Issue certiﬁcate with the input parameter
grade.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains specialized UML activity diagram actions: event-based actions and call behavior actions. Event-based actions include ''accept event'' (concave pentagon) for waiting on signals, ''accept time event'' (hourglass) for time-based triggers, and ''send signal'' (convex pentagon) for transmissions. Call behavior actions, identified by an inverted fork symbol, enable hierarchical modeling by referencing external activities. This allows complex sub-processes to be treated as single atomic units within a high-level diagram, while their detailed internal steps are defined elsewhere.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What are the visual notation differences between an accept event action, a send signal action, and an accept time event action?
2. What does the inverted fork symbol signify when it appears in an action rectangle?
3. In the context of call behavior actions, why is an action like ''Issue certificate'' treated as an atomic unit in the parent activity?
4. How can a modeler show the detailed internal steps of a call behavior action without cluttering the main activity diagram?','e9eec55f-02ae-444d-906c-025dfdad1cdc'::uuid,NULL,NULL,155,680,'2026-05-08 16:23:24.148119+07');
