edge to allow a certain number of tokens to be consumed at that edge
by a single execution. The weight of an edge is speciﬁed in curly brack-
ets with the keyword weight. It is always a whole number greater than
or equal to zero. If the weight is zero, this means that all tokens present
are consumed. Alternatively, all or ∗can also be written instead of zero.
If no weight is speciﬁed, 1 is assumed as the default value. Figure 7.5
gives an example of the use of weights. If the signal Register is received
30 times, meaning that at least 30 students have registered and thus 30
tokens are offered to the subsequent action, then this subsequent action
is executed, consumes 30 tokens, and a new group is created.
