tivity ﬁnal nodes in one
activity diagram
If you only want to end one execution path, leaving the other concur-
rently active execution paths unaffected, you have to use the ﬂow ﬁnal
Flow ﬁnal node
node. This node only deletes the tokens that ﬂow into it directly, thus
ending just the respective path. All other tokens of the activity remain
unaffected and may continue to exist. The ﬂow ﬁnal node is represented
by a small circle containing an X and has only incoming edges.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment details control nodes used to manage the termination of activities in UML activity diagrams. It introduces the combined parallelization and synchronization node for complex branching. It distinguishes between the Activity Final Node, which terminates the entire activity and all concurrent subpaths upon receiving a token, and the Flow Final Node, which only terminates a single execution path while allowing others to continue. It also explains that reaching any one of multiple Activity Final Nodes results in the immediate end of the entire activity.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the functional difference between an Activity Final Node and a Flow Final Node regarding concurrent paths?
2. How does the system respond when a diagram contains multiple Activity Final Nodes and one is reached?
3. What visual symbols are used to distinguish an Activity Final Node from a Flow Final Node?
4. Under what circumstances would you use a combined synchronization and parallelization node?','a76de159-ad7b-4493-b5d2-78b5cb86d5ce'::uuid,NULL,NULL,161,795,'2026-05-08 16:23:24.148805+07');
