7.5 Partitions
A partition allows you to group nodes and edges of an activity based on
Partition
A
B
common properties. If we consider a business process, for example, we
could use a partition to group all actions that a speciﬁc entity is respon-
sible for executing. UML has no strict rules regarding the grouping cri-
teria that can be used. Generally, partitions reﬂect organizational units

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains two types of object nodes and the concept of partitions in activity diagrams. A central buffer acts as transient memory where data tokens are consumed and deleted upon use. In contrast, a data store serves as permanent memory, allowing tokens to be copied and reused multiple times. The text also introduces partitions (swimlanes), which are used to group nodes and edges based on shared properties like organizational units. While partitions clarify responsibilities, they do not alter the activity''s execution semantics.

