7.7 Concluding Example
To sum up, we look at the process that has to be executed for a future
student of a university to receive a student identiﬁcation card (student
ID). We use an activity diagram to model this process. In textual form,
the process of issuing a student ID can be described as follows: To ob-
tain a student ID, the student must request this ID from an employee of
the student ofﬁce. The employee hands the student the forms that the
student has to ﬁll out to register at the university. These forms include
the student ID itself, which is a small, old-style cardboard card. The

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the concept of an interruptible activity region in UML activity diagrams, where a group of actions can be terminated immediately upon a specific event. It uses a student withdrawal scenario to illustrate how control tokens are deleted and redirected. Additionally, it introduces a comprehensive case study on the process of issuing a university student ID, detailing the interactions between students and employees, including form requests and registration procedures.

