package diagrams
9.1.2 Importing Elements/Packages
Elements of a speciﬁc package can reference one another and communicate with one another without any further details provided this is not restricted by visibilities and navigation directions. For example, an element E that is located in a package P1 can also be used in a package P2 provided P2 does not contain an element with the same name E and P2 is included directly or indirectly in P1. Elements from other packages can either be imported or referenced using qualiﬁed names. All elements of the imported package with the corresponding visibility become visible in the importing package. These elements can thus be referenced directly. The name of an imported element is added to the namespace of the package and can then be used without qualiﬁcation (that is, without namespace::).
Think back to the class diagram (Chapter 4)—there we deﬁned visibilities of attributes, operations, and roles. In doing so, we also became familiar with the visibility package, notated by ˜ (see Tab. 4.1 on page 59). This visibility means that the attributes, operations, and roles are only visible for elements within the same package.
Import relationships are denoted by a dashed arrow that points away from the importer and is labeled with «import». Of course, only elements that are visible externally can be imported, such as class C1 in package P3, which is imported by package P1 (Fig. 9.2).

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the visual notation of UML packages and the mechanism for importing elements. Packages act as namespaces to organize models. Elements within a package can be referenced using qualified names or brought into another namespace via an import relationship. The segment details three notation alternatives for packages and explains that an import (represented by a dashed arrow with the «import» keyword) allows elements to be used without their namespace prefix, provided they have the necessary external visibility.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What are the three notation alternatives for representing the contents of a package in a UML diagram?
2. What is the difference between referencing an element using a qualified name versus using an imported name?
3. How does the package visibility (notated by ~) restrict the use of attributes or operations?
4. What specific graphical notation and label are used to denote an import relationship between two packages?','5c1fa3db-a69d-48de-b193-0dea72f8f733'::uuid,NULL,NULL,195,646,'2026-05-08 16:23:24.152482+07');
