INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('de15c79f-8e30-4d6a-a551-714f3a0526b8'::uuid,'cbb8c449-2e22-4cda-ae7c-854b156c561a'::uuid,3,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
What is Polymorphism?
In Java, polymorphism refers to the fact that you can have multiple
methods with the same name in the same class.
4
Two kinds of polymorphism:
●
Overloading: two or more methods with different signatures.
●
Overriding: replacing an inherited method with another having the
same signature.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment defines polymorphism in Java as the ability to define multiple methods with the same name within a class. It identifies two primary forms: overloading and overriding. Overloading involves creating multiple methods with the same name but distinct signatures (different parameters). Overriding occurs when a subclass provides a new implementation for an inherited method while maintaining the exact same signature. These concepts are fundamental to achieving flexibility and reusability in object-oriented programming.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. How does Java define the concept of polymorphism regarding method naming?
2. What are the two specific types of polymorphism described in the text?
3. What is the requirement for method signatures when performing method overloading?
4. In the context of inheritance, how is method overriding defined?','00292e62-402a-4553-a3d2-b4ef853f2afc'::uuid,NULL,NULL,4,303,'2026-05-08 16:24:56.701787+07');
