9.4 Model-Based Software Development
Models often serve as construction plans for software. For the develop-
ers, they stipulate which properties the end product should have. They
specify the requirements that a software system must fulﬁll and describe
which components occur in the system and how these components inter-
act. Models are thus the basis for the development of executable code,
which is traditionally created by human programmers. If the model and
the code are to be kept up-to-date, any changes made must be imple-
mented in both the model and the code. This involves additional effort.
The next step in simplifying the software development process is
obvious—namely the automatic generation of the code from the models.
This brings us to model-based software development. Models are sig-
niﬁcantly more than pretty pictures for documenting a software system
and for use as sketches or blueprints. In model-based software develop-
ment, the executable system is created from the models. Here, therefore,
models have the same value as code.
In model-based software development (MBSD), source code is cre-
Model-based software
development
ated partially or completely from models. Programming is thus replaced
by modeling. The models must describe the function of the system to
be developed as precisely as possible. The semantics of the modeling

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the application of stereotypes via the «apply» relationship and introduces Model-Based Software Development (MBSD). In MBSD, models transcend their traditional role as mere blueprints; they are treated with the same value as executable code. By replacing manual programming with modeling, MBSD enables the automatic generation of source code from models. This approach reduces the manual effort required to keep design and implementation synchronized, provided the models describe system functions with high precision and clear semantics.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. According to Figure 9.7, what notation and keyword are used to indicate that a package is using stereotypes defined in a specific profile?
2. What is the main disadvantage mentioned regarding the traditional relationship between models and manually written code?
3. How does the role of a model change in Model-Based Software Development (MBSD) compared to its role as a traditional ''blueprint''?
4. Why is it critical for models to describe the system as precisely as possible in the context of MBSD?','cfa429c7-388c-4d5a-9f4a-a20e3d0035ea'::uuid,NULL,NULL,202,699,'2026-05-08 16:23:24.153283+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('691924d7-da5a-415f-a10c-8a695f18ad8d'::uuid,'f0f814ac-a731-4f96-83e3-c1a59ee880d5'::uuid,201,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
9.4 Model-Based Software Development
193
languages used must be deﬁned unambiguously so that the language
elements can be transformed into code uniquely. General purpose mod-
General purpose
modeling language
eling languages such as UML may be very ﬂexible but they are often
too general for speciﬁc applications, meaning that speciﬁc concepts are
missing. For example, in the description of a web application, the “hy-
perlink” is a central concept that is not available in UML. Therefore, in
model-based software development, smaller languages tailored to the
respective domain are often used. These languages are also referred to
as domain-speciﬁc languages (DSL). Of course, they can be deﬁned
Domain-speciﬁc
language (DSL)
based on UML, as we saw in the previous section on stereotypes and
proﬁles.
In MBSD, models are transformed into other models that, for exam-
ple, contain speciﬁc details about the target platform of the system. Al-
ternatively, executable code is generated in common programming lan-
guages. The expert knowledge that is required to create an executable
program is therefore invested in these model transformations. It is thus
retained in an infrastructure that can be reused again. In recent years,
numerous special languages and frameworks have been introduced for
creating these transformations.
The aim of MBSD is to signiﬁcantly simplify the development of
applications by replacing programming with modeling, without the de-
velopers or modelers having to have speciﬁc knowledge about the plat-
forms used.
To summarize, model-based software development aims to provide
the following advantages [49]:
• Increase the speed of development: code, which is often repeated, is
created automatically and adapted to the current situation. There is a
signiﬁcant decrease in “copy and paste” activities.
• Increase the quality of the software: the automatic transformations
reduce the risk of errors in the implementation. The code is also cre-
ated independently of the abilities and experience of a developer.
Thanks to the tool support, the implementation of the software ar-
chitecture is better.
• Central troubleshooting: errors only have to be corrected in the
model and they are then eliminated in the corresponding code parts
when the transformation is executed again. The transformation rules
may have to be adapted if they no longer match the requirements.
However, this only has to be done once.
• Increase the reuse: modeling languages, in particular DSLs, only
have to be created once. They aggregate expert knowledge that can
then be used in different projects.

=== SUMMARY ===
Model-Based Software Development (MBSD) shifts the focus from manual coding to modeling, using models as the primary basis for generating executable code. To ensure successful transformation, modeling languages must be unambiguous. While UML is a flexible general-purpose language, Domain-Specific Languages (DSLs) are often used to address specific domain needs. MBSD offers several benefits, including faster development through automation, higher software quality via reduced manual errors, centralized troubleshooting within the model, and increased reuse of expert knowledge stored in transformation infrastructures.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the primary objective of Model-Based Software Development (MBSD) regarding the role of programming?
2. Why might a Domain-Specific Language (DSL) be preferred over a general-purpose language like UML in certain projects?
3. How does the use of model transformations contribute to the reuse of expert knowledge?
4. Name three specific advantages of MBSD mentioned in the text and explain how they improve the development process.','16a421e0-46ee-48e9-b0be-3d06e8bdd60a'::uuid,NULL,NULL,203,933,'2026-05-08 16:23:24.153425+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('40ec3198-6cc3-4ee8-b6aa-98b326679ca8'::uuid,'f0f814ac-a731-4f96-83e3-c1a59ee880d5'::uuid,202,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
194
9 Further Topics
• Handling complexity through abstraction: the complexity of the im-
plementation language remains hidden in the modeling languages;
technical implementation details do not have to be considered.
• Portability: the code can be generated for different platforms from
one model.
Models are used not only to generate the implementation of the sys-
tem to be developed; they are also used for simulations, for analyzing
system properties, for generating test cases, and for verifying and vali-
dating the system to be developed. Model-based software development
can be implemented in different ways. For a detailed introduction to this
topic, see [6, 49].

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment highlights key advantages of Model-Based Software Development (MBSD), focusing on abstraction and portability. By hiding technical implementation details, MBSD allows developers to manage complexity more effectively. It also enables the generation of code for multiple platforms from a single model. Furthermore, the text explains that models serve diverse purposes beyond code generation, including system simulation, property analysis, test case generation, and system verification/validation.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. How does abstraction in model-based software development help manage the complexity of the implementation language?
2. What is meant by ''portability'' in the context of MBSD models?
3. Besides code generation, what are four other ways models can be utilized during the development process?
4. Why is it advantageous that technical implementation details do not have to be considered by the modeler?','cf6264fa-28c3-4bfc-acb7-973758ad3304'::uuid,NULL,NULL,204,412,'2026-05-08 16:23:24.153507+07');
