INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('b3915831-3cd3-4338-877a-17cd6f5feefd'::uuid,'1bd88230-19ac-4d5a-93f1-44733cf34dd3'::uuid,6,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
KEY CONCEPTS OF
INHERITANCE
02
7

=== SUMMARY ===
This section introduces the fundamental principles of inheritance in Object-Oriented Programming. It explores how a derived class inherits attributes and behaviors from a base class, establishing an ''is-a'' relationship. The content highlights key benefits such as code reusability and extensibility, which simplify maintenance and updates. Furthermore, it covers essential mechanics including method overriding and the specific execution sequence of constructors and destructors within a class hierarchy, providing a foundation for understanding complex relationships between objects.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. How does inheritance improve the efficiency and maintainability of a codebase?
2. What is the difference between a base class and a derived class in terms of properties and methods?
3. What does it mean for a derived class to ''override'' a method from its base class?
4. In a class hierarchy, why is the order of constructor and destructor execution important when an object is created or destroyed?','a259973f-49ee-433e-b4c2-6499e7e0531b'::uuid,NULL,NULL,7,271,'2026-05-08 16:25:28.48245+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('17f14b43-f1dc-4ccd-a177-d1934ce602d7'::uuid,'1bd88230-19ac-4d5a-93f1-44733cf34dd3'::uuid,7,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Base class & Derived class
8
Base class (Parent/Superclass): The class whose properties (attributes)
and methods (functions) are inherited by another class.
Example: Animal is a base class.
Derived class (Child/Subclass): The class
inheriting from the base class. It can use or
override the members of the base class
and add its own ⇒“is-a” relationship
Example: Dog inherits from Animal ⇒Dog
“is-a” Animal.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment defines the core components of inheritance: the Base class (Parent) and the Derived class (Child). The Base class provides properties and methods, while the Derived class inherits these features, can override them, and adds its own specific functionality. This structure creates an ''is-a'' relationship, as seen in the example where a Dog ''is-a'' type of Animal, demonstrating how classes are logically linked in a hierarchy.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What are the common synonyms used for Base class and Derived class?
2. What actions can a Derived class perform on the members it inherits from a Base class?
3. What does the ''is-a'' relationship represent in the context of inheritance?
4. If a ''Car'' class inherits from a ''Vehicle'' class, which one acts as the Superclass and why?','dfdd1eec-632b-443e-8382-bf542779947b'::uuid,NULL,NULL,8,311,'2026-05-08 16:25:28.482525+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('2a1843d6-677b-4619-87ba-cb1617c25a7b'::uuid,'1bd88230-19ac-4d5a-93f1-44733cf34dd3'::uuid,8,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Method overriding
A derived class can redefine (override) a method from the base class to
provide specific functionality.
●The method in the base class must have the same name, return
type, and parameters as the overridden method.
9
Constructors: When an object of a derived class is created, the base
class constructor is called first, followed by the derived class
constructor.
Destructors: When an object is destroyed, the derived class destructor
is called first, followed by the base class destructor.
Introduced more detailed in Chap 3 - Polymorphism

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains method overriding, where a derived class redefines a base class method with the same signature to provide specific functionality. It also details the execution order of class members during an object''s lifecycle: constructors are called in order from base class to derived class, while destructors execute in the reverse order, starting from the derived class and moving up to the base class.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What criteria must a method meet to successfully override a base class method?
2. What is the sequence of constructor execution when a derived class object is created?
3. In what order are destructors called when a derived class object is destroyed?
4. Why would a developer choose to override a method in a derived class?','0ac9af81-5540-4d6b-ae27-854e9b854a24'::uuid,NULL,NULL,9,341,'2026-05-08 16:25:28.482584+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('eed3dc7b-b019-482c-9e71-4e2f713e19c3'::uuid,'1bd88230-19ac-4d5a-93f1-44733cf34dd3'::uuid,9,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Types of Inheritance
Single Inheritance: A derived class inherits from one base class.
Multiple Inheritance: A class inherits from more than one base class.
(Supported in C++ but not in Java)
Multilevel Inheritance: A class inherits from a derived class, forming a
chain.
Hierarchical Inheritance: Multiple derived classes inherit from one base
class.
Hybrid Inheritance: A combination of two or more types of inheritance.
10

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment outlines the five primary models of inheritance in object-oriented programming. It defines Single, Multiple, Multilevel, Hierarchical, and Hybrid inheritance, explaining how classes can derive properties from one or more base classes to form various structures. It specifically notes that while C++ supports Multiple Inheritance, Java does not, highlighting language-specific implementation differences in how class hierarchies are constructed.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the fundamental difference between Single Inheritance and Multiple Inheritance?
2. Which specific programming language mentioned in the text does not support Multiple Inheritance?
3. How does Multilevel Inheritance differ from Hierarchical Inheritance in terms of class structure?
4. How would you define Hybrid Inheritance in the context of the other inheritance types?','e2bf85cf-3a0d-4a84-aaa6-062c132cd6c2'::uuid,NULL,NULL,10,333,'2026-05-08 16:25:28.482645+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('62ddb978-a0f8-48f5-a386-0f6d703f089d'::uuid,'1bd88230-19ac-4d5a-93f1-44733cf34dd3'::uuid,10,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Types of Inheritance
11
Diamond
Problem?

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces the ''Diamond Problem,'' a critical concept in object-oriented programming related to multiple inheritance. It occurs when a subclass inherits from two intermediate classes that both derive from a single base class, creating ambiguity in which base class members to use. This problem is a primary reason why some languages, like Java, do not support multiple inheritance of classes, while others, like C++, provide specific mechanisms to resolve such conflicts.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the ''Diamond Problem'' in the context of object-oriented programming?
2. Which specific type of inheritance is responsible for creating the Diamond Problem?
3. Why does the Diamond Problem cause ambiguity during method or member access?
4. How does the Diamond Problem influence the design of inheritance in languages like Java compared to C++?','0ae86cc9-1e34-4f87-a11c-142ee0c1ed7e'::uuid,NULL,NULL,11,236,'2026-05-08 16:25:28.482679+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('79dec41a-11f1-4b3b-bb3b-99048dca858f'::uuid,'1bd88230-19ac-4d5a-93f1-44733cf34dd3'::uuid,12,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Subclass inherits all members from Superclass
When a subclass inherits from a superclass:
●All members of the superclass, whether public or private, are
technically inherited.
●However, accessibility depends on the access specifier:
○
Public and protected members can be directly accessed in the
subclass.
○
Private members are inherited but not accessible directly; they
are accessible through public or protected getters and setters
in the superclass.
13

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains how inheritance affects the accessibility of class members. While a subclass technically inherits all members from its superclass (including private ones), access is restricted by specifiers. Public and protected members are directly accessible in the subclass. In contrast, private members are inherited but cannot be accessed directly; they must be reached indirectly through the superclass''s public or protected methods, such as getters and setters. This maintains encapsulation while allowing for code reuse.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. Does a subclass technically inherit private members from its superclass?
2. Which access specifiers allow for direct access of members within a subclass?
3. How can a subclass interact with private members inherited from a superclass?
4. What determines the accessibility of inherited members in a subclass?','cba243e0-9fef-4f00-82f5-549d272d0db0'::uuid,NULL,NULL,13,342,'2026-05-08 16:25:28.482776+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('d1b74c21-fd3a-4fb0-8a6e-6e059f4ee94c'::uuid,'1bd88230-19ac-4d5a-93f1-44733cf34dd3'::uuid,13,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Subclass inherits all members from Superclass
14

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment highlights a fundamental rule of inheritance: a subclass inherits all members from its superclass, including public, protected, and private elements. However, inheritance does not grant equal access to all members. While public and protected members are directly accessible, private members are inherited but remain inaccessible directly. Instead, they must be managed through the superclass''s public or protected interface, such as getter and setter methods, ensuring that encapsulation principles are preserved within the class hierarchy.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. Do subclasses technically inherit private members from their superclass?
