INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('333f1af0-8c1b-4880-9c89-9b3ca07ab19f'::uuid,'1bd88230-19ac-4d5a-93f1-44733cf34dd3'::uuid,14,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Pointers for Polymorphism (1)
class Superclass {
public:
virtual void display() { cout << "Superclass\n"; }
};
class Subclass : public Superclass {
public:
void display() override { cout << "Subclass\n"; }
};
int main() {
Subclass sub;
Superclass sup = sub;
sup.display(); // Output: ???
}
15
Superclass
Subclass
sup
sub

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment illustrates the concept of ''object slicing'' in C++. It demonstrates that even when a method is marked as virtual, assigning a derived class object to a base class object by value results in the loss of polymorphic behavior. In this specific example, the base class version of the method is called because the object ''sup'' is a value-type Superclass object, not a pointer or reference, meaning it only contains the base portion of the Subclass instance.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the output of the code provided in the target segment, and why?
2. Explain the concept of ''object slicing'' as it applies to the assignment ''Superclass sup = sub;''.
3. Despite the ''display'' method being virtual, why does polymorphism fail to occur in this specific main function?
4. How must the variable ''sup'' be declared to ensure that ''Subclass'' is printed instead of ''Superclass''?','5b6de6d7-9117-4f2c-ad6a-160b82366163'::uuid,NULL,NULL,15,312,'2026-05-08 16:25:28.482855+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('ab6c769d-b881-4779-9bed-147d0af93ac2'::uuid,'1bd88230-19ac-4d5a-93f1-44733cf34dd3'::uuid,15,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Pointers for Polymorphism (2)
class Superclass {
public:
virtual void display() { cout << "Superclass\n"; }
};
class Subclass : public Superclass {
public:
void display() override { cout << "Subclass\n"; }
};
int main() {
Subclass sub;
Superclass* sup = &sub;
sup–>display(); // Output: ???
}
16
Superclass
Subclass
sup
sub

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment demonstrates C++ polymorphism using pointers. It illustrates how a base class pointer can point to a derived class object. Because the ''display'' method is declared as ''virtual'' in the Superclass, C++ uses dynamic dispatch to call the overridden version in the Subclass at runtime. Unlike object slicing where the derived part is lost, using a pointer allows the program to access the specific behavior of the Subclass, resulting in the output ''Subclass''.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the expected output of the code when ''sup->display()'' is called?
2. Why is it possible for a ''Superclass*'' pointer to store the address of a ''Subclass'' object?
