In Java (and many other OOP
languages): All objects are accessed via
references (similar to pointers in C++).

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains that in Java, objects are managed through references, which function similarly to pointers in C++. It demonstrates polymorphism where a superclass reference points to a subclass instance. When a method is called on this reference, Java uses dynamic method dispatch to execute the overridden version in the subclass. This highlights how Java achieves polymorphic behavior implicitly through its reference-based memory model, ensuring that the actual object''s type determines which method is executed at runtime.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
