=== SUMMARY ===
This chapter introduces the Decorator Pattern, which allows developers to add new responsibilities to objects at runtime using composition instead of inheritance. By exploring the Open-Closed Principle, the text explains how to extend functionality without modifying existing code. Using the Starbuzz Coffee case study, it demonstrates how to wrap objects with decorators to dynamically build complex behaviors. Finally, it highlights real-world applications of the pattern within the Java I/O library, emphasizing the flexibility of runtime extension over compile-time subclassing.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the primary difference between extending functionality via inheritance versus using the Decorator Pattern?
2. How does the Decorator Pattern support the ''Open-Closed Principle'' mentioned in the text?
3. According to the chapter, why is runtime extension preferred over compile-time extension in certain design scenarios?
4. What is a real-world example of the Decorator Pattern provided in the text beyond the Starbuzz Coffee example?','75ed6b84-99cf-4216-b634-7090ba16fab7'::uuid,NULL,NULL,15,539,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.913754+07');
