=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces the Singleton Pattern, a design pattern used to ensure a class has only one instance while providing a global point of access. Although it features a simple class diagram consisting of just a single class, the implementation requires careful object-oriented consideration, especially regarding multithreading. The chapter explores the classic implementation through a ''Chocolate Factory'' case study, addressing common pitfalls like synchronization issues to ensure that the ''one-of-a-kind'' nature of the object is maintained across different execution environments.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the primary purpose of the Singleton Pattern according to the text?
2. Why is the Singleton Pattern described as having the simplest class diagram among all design patterns?
3. What specific challenge arises when implementing a Singleton in a multithreaded application?
4. How does the Singleton Pattern differ from the Factory Method in terms of its core intent?','f47f770d-859a-4495-a18f-9ff9a06f4c78'::uuid,NULL,NULL,17,700,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.914001+07');
