=== SUMMARY ===
This chapter focuses on the Adapter and Facade patterns, which both wrap objects but for different purposes. The Adapter pattern converts one interface into another to help incompatible classes work together. The Facade pattern provides a simplified interface to a complex set of classes in a subsystem. Additionally, the chapter introduces the ''Principle of Least Knowledge,'' which teaches how to reduce dependencies between objects. By the end, readers understand how to adapt designs and simplify complex system interactions through effective encapsulation.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the primary difference between the Adapter Pattern and the Facade Pattern in terms of their intent?
2. In the context of the Adapter Pattern, what roles do the ''Client'', ''Adapter'', and ''Adaptee'' play?
3. How does the Facade Pattern help in following the ''Principle of Least Knowledge''?
4. Why might a developer choose to use an Adapter instead of simply modifying the original class''s code?','3021da42-1daa-4be7-9a90-f4d1c2532cff'::uuid,NULL,NULL,19,694,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.914247+07');
