=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces the Iterator and Composite design patterns. The Iterator Pattern provides a way to access elements of an aggregate object sequentially without exposing its underlying representation. This encourages encapsulation and adheres to the Single Responsibility Principle. The Composite Pattern allows you to compose objects into tree structures to represent part-whole hierarchies, enabling clients to treat individual objects and compositions of objects uniformly. Together, these patterns simplify the management and traversal of complex object collections and hierarchical data structures.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the primary benefit of using the Iterator Pattern when dealing with different collection types like Arrays and ArrayLists?
2. How does the Iterator Pattern help maintain the Single Responsibility Principle within a class?
3. In the context of the Composite Pattern, what does it mean to treat individual objects and compositions uniformly?
4. What role does the ''MenuComponent'' play in the implementation of a hierarchical menu system using the Composite Pattern?','3043844a-8221-4f46-b32b-bb598994d4cd'::uuid,NULL,NULL,21,848,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.914496+07');
