=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces the State Pattern, illustrating how it allows objects to change their behavior based on internal state transitions. While structurally similar to the Strategy Pattern, the State Pattern has a different intent: managing state-dependent behavior rather than interchangeable algorithms. Using a Gumball Machine case study, the text explores state machines, transitioning from a basic implementation to a flexible, maintainable design. It covers defining state interfaces, reworking classes, and handling complex change requests to ensure the system remains robust as new behaviors are added.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
