12	 	 Chapter 1
program to an interface
The word interface is overloaded here. There’s the concept of an
interface, but there’s also the Java construct of an interface. You
can program to an interface without having to actually use a Java
interface. The point is to exploit polymorphism by programming
to a supertype so that the actual runtime object isn’t locked into
the code. And we could rephrase “program to a supertype” as
“the declared type of the variables should be a supertype, usually
an abstract class or interface, so that the objects assigned to
those variables can be of any concrete implementation of the
supertype, which means the class declaring them doesn’t have to
know about the actual object types!”
This is probably old news to you, but just to make sure we’re
all saying the same thing, here’s a simple example of using a
polymorphic type—imagine an abstract class Animal, with two
concrete implementations, Dog and Cat.
Programming to an implementation would be:
Dog d = new Dog();
d.bark();
But programming to an interface/supertype would be:
Animal animal = new Dog();
animal.makeSound();
Even better, rather than hardcoding the instantiation of the
subtype (like new Dog()) into the code, assign the concrete
implementation object at runtime:
a = getAnimal();
a.makeSound();
“Program to an interface” really means
“Program to a supertype.”
makeSound()
Animal
makeSound() {
bark();
}
bark() { // bark sound }
Dog
makeSound() {
meow();
}
meow() { // meow sound }
Cat
Abstract supertype (could
be an abstract class OR
interface).
Concrete
implementations.
Declaring the variable “d” as type
Dog (a concrete implementation
of Animal) forces us to code to a
concrete implementation.
We know it’s a Dog, but
we can now use the animal
reference polymorphically.
We don’t know WHAT the actual
animal subtype is...all we care about
is that it knows how to respond to
makeSound().
I don’t see why you
have to use an interface for
FlyBehavior. You can do the
same thing with an abstract
superclass. Isn’t the whole point
to use polymorphism?

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the key design principle: "Program to an interface, not an implementation." It clarifies that an "interface" in this context refers to any supertype, such as an abstract class or a formal interface. By declaring variable types as supertypes, developers can exploit polymorphism, ensuring the code is not locked into specific concrete objects. This approach allows the actual implementation to be assigned at runtime, providing flexibility and decoupling the class from specific object types.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the difference between the conceptual ''interface'' and the Java ''interface'' construct in the context of this design principle?
2. Why is it considered restrictive to declare a variable using a concrete implementation type like ''Dog''?
3. How does programming to a supertype (like ''Animal'') allow for polymorphic behavior in code?
4. What is the benefit of assigning a concrete implementation object at runtime instead of hardcoding ''new Dog()''?','12b279da-4bd9-4165-8e84-28ea15e79104'::uuid,NULL,NULL,50,789,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.917772+07');
