INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('634e5363-ee2d-4152-b4b6-9337bf249d13'::uuid,'56576a1d-5b81-4e06-824c-2a0e45e5a305'::uuid,53,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Wait a second, didn’t you
say we should NOT program to an
implementation? But what are we doing in that
constructor? We’re making a new instance of a
concrete Quack implementation class!

Good catch, that’s exactly what we’re doing...
for now.
Later in the book we’ll have more patterns in
our toolbox that can help us fix it.
Still, notice that while we are setting the
behaviors to concrete classes (by instantiating
a behavior class like Quack or FlyWithWings
and assigning it to our behavior reference
variable), we could easily change that at
runtime.
So, we still have a lot of flexibility here. That
said, we’re doing a poor job of initializing
the instance variables in a flexible way. But
think about it: since the quackBehavior
instance variable is an interface type, we
could (through the magic of polymorphism)
dynamically assign a different QuackBehavior
implementation class at runtime.
Take a moment and think about how you
would implement a duck so that its behavior
could change at runtime. (You’ll see the code
that does this a few pages from now.)

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment addresses a potential contradiction: instantiating concrete classes in a constructor despite the rule against programming to an implementation. It explains that while current initialization is rigid, using interface types for behavior variables allows for flexibility. Through polymorphism, these behaviors can be dynamically reassigned at runtime. The text acknowledges this temporary design flaw and hints that future patterns will provide better ways to initialize these variables while maintaining the ability to change behaviors on the fly.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. Why does the author acknowledge that they are currently ''programming to an implementation'' in the duck''s constructor?
2. How does using an interface type for the ''quackBehavior'' variable provide flexibility even if it is initially set to a concrete class?
3. What specific programming concept allows for the dynamic assignment of different behavior implementations at runtime?
4. What is the suggested goal for improving the duck''s implementation in the upcoming pages of the book?','afb04645-74d7-4f53-9334-4d14793534a4'::uuid,NULL,NULL,55,544,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.918249+07');
