INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('6074786f-ebeb-491e-b145-76e9a4280702'::uuid,'56576a1d-5b81-4e06-824c-2a0e45e5a305'::uuid,80,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
44	 	 Chapter 2
meet the observer pattern
Meet the Observer Pattern
You know how newspaper or magazine
subscriptions work:
A newspaper publisher goes into business and begins
publishing newspapers.
You subscribe to a particular publisher, and every time
there’s a new edition it gets delivered to you. As long as
you remain a subscriber, you get new newspapers.
You unsubscribe when you don’t want papers anymore,
and they stop being delivered.
While the publisher remains in business, people, hotels,
airlines, and other businesses constantly subscribe and
unsubscribe to the newspaper.
1
2
3
4
No way we want to
miss what’s going on in
Objectville. Of course we
subscribe.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces the Observer Pattern using a newspaper subscription analogy. It describes a system where a publisher (subject) sends updates to its subscribers (observers). As long as an entity is subscribed, they receive new editions automatically. They can stop receiving updates at any time by unsubscribing. This illustrates the dynamic one-to-many relationship between a data source and the multiple parties interested in its state changes, allowing for flexible addition and removal of observers at runtime.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. In the newspaper analogy, which role represents the ''Subject'' and which represents the ''Observer''?
2. What triggers the delivery of a newspaper to a subscriber?
3. What happens to the flow of information when a person or business decides to unsubscribe?
4. How does the analogy describe the flexibility of the subscription list over time?','7046aede-208b-46fe-8dc9-1a41e573e20d'::uuid,NULL,NULL,82,401,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.920842+07');
