Five-minute drama: a subject for observation
In today’s skit, two enterprising software developers encounter a real
live head hunter...
This is Lori. I’m looking
for a Java development
position. I’ve got five years
of experience and...
Uh, yeah, you and
everybody else, baby.
I’m putting you on my list of
Java developers. Don’t call
me, I’ll call you!
Hi, I’m Jill. I’ve written
a lot of enterprise systems.
I’m interested in any job you’ve
got with Java development.
I’ll add you to the list—
you’ll know along with
everyone else.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces the Observer Pattern through a real-world analogy involving a headhunter and job seekers. The headhunter acts as the ''Subject,'' maintaining a list of interested software developers, who are the ''Observers.'' Instead of developers constantly checking for updates, they register their interest once. The headhunter then takes responsibility for notifying all registered developers simultaneously when a new job becomes available. This illustrates the pattern''s core concept: a one-to-many dependency where state changes are pushed from a central subject to multiple observers.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. In this analogy, identify which character acts as the Subject and which characters act as the Observers.
2. What does the headhunter''s ''list'' represent in the technical implementation of the Observer Pattern?
3. Explain how the phrase ''Don''t call me, I''ll call you!'' relates to the communication flow between a Subject and its Observers.
4. What must Lori or Jill do if they no longer want to receive notifications from the headhunter?','331b1093-9ac7-4991-828b-8f2881c0cf60'::uuid,NULL,NULL,86,433,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.921106+07');
