ConcreteObserver
The Observer Pattern: the Class Diagram
The concrete subject may also
have methods for setting and
getting its state (more about
this later).
observers
subject
Each subject
can have many
observers.
Q: What does this have to do with
one-to-many relationships?
A: With the Observer Pattern, the Subject
is the object that contains the state and
controls it. So, there is ONE subject with
state. The observers, on the other hand, use
the state, even if they don’t own it. There
are many observers, and they rely on the
Subject to tell them when its state changes.
So there is a relationship between the ONE
Subject to the MANY Observers.
Q: How does dependence come into
this?
A: Because the subject is the sole owner
of that data, the observers are dependent on
the subject to update them when the data
changes. This leads to a cleaner OO design
than allowing many objects to control the
same data.
Q: I’ve also heard of a Publish-
Subscribe Pattern. Is that just another
name for the Observer Pattern?
A: No, although they are related. The
Publish-Subscribe pattern is a more complex
pattern that allows subscribers to express
interest in different types of messages
and further separates publishers from
subscribers. It is often used in middleware
systems.
Let’s take a look at the structure of the Observer Pattern, complete with
its Subject and Observer classes. Here’s the class diagram:

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment illustrates the class diagram of the Observer Pattern, detailing the roles of the Subject and Observer interfaces. It explains how a single Subject manages state and triggers updates for multiple Observers through a one-to-many relationship. By ensuring that the Subject is the sole owner of data, the pattern creates a cleaner object-oriented design where observers depend on the subject for updates. It also clarifies the distinction between the Observer Pattern and the more complex Publish-Subscribe Pattern used in middleware.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What are the three essential methods that a Subject interface must define to manage its relationship with observers?
2. How does the use of an Observer interface allow the Subject to interact with various concrete observer classes?
3. In the context of the Observer Pattern, why is the Subject considered the ''owner'' of the state while Observers are considered ''dependents''?
4. How does the Publish-Subscribe Pattern differ from the standard Observer Pattern in terms of how publishers and subscribers interact?','30ca8654-9238-4d02-af50-b7a5d385a40e'::uuid,NULL,NULL,90,878,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.921439+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('aa7e6baa-ff89-41a0-9623-e4788c80a411'::uuid,'56576a1d-5b81-4e06-824c-2a0e45e5a305'::uuid,89,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
you are here 4    53
the observer pattern
Guru and Student...
Guru: Have we talked about loose coupling?
Student: Guru, I do not recall such a discussion.
Guru: Is a tightly woven basket stiff or flexible?
Student: Stiff, Guru.
Guru: And do stiff or flexible baskets tear or break less easily?
Student: A flexible basket tends to break less easily.
Guru: And in our software, might our designs break less easily if
our objects are less tightly bound together?
Student: Guru, I see the truth of it. But what does it mean for
objects to be less tightly bound?
Guru: We like to call it, loosely coupled.
Student: Ah!
Guru: We say a object is tightly coupled to another object when it is
too dependent on that object.
Student: So a loosely coupled object can’t depend on another
object?
Guru: Think of nature; all living things depend on each other.
Likewise, all objects depend on other objects. But a loosely coupled
object doesn’t know or care too much about the details of another
object.
Student: But Guru, that doesn’t sound like a good quality. Surely
not knowing is worse than knowing.
Guru: You are doing well in your studies, but you have much to
learn. By not knowing too much about other objects, we can create
designs that can handle change better. Designs that have more
flexibility, like the less tightly woven basket.
Student: Of course, I am sure you are right. Could you give me an
example?
Guru: That is enough for today.

=== SUMMARY ===
This dialogue introduces the concept of loose coupling using the analogy of a woven basket. It explains that while objects in software naturally depend on one another, ''tightly coupled'' objects are too dependent on specific details, making the system rigid and prone to breaking. In contrast, ''loosely coupled'' designs involve objects that interact without needing intimate knowledge of each other''s internals. This approach creates more flexible systems that can handle change more effectively, much like a flexible basket is less likely to tear than a stiff one.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. According to the Guru''s analogy, why is a flexible basket superior to a stiff, tightly woven one?
2. How does the Guru define ''tightly coupled'' objects in software design?
3. Why does the Student initially think that ''not knowing'' about another object''s details is a bad quality?
4. What is the primary benefit of creating designs where objects do not know too much about each other?','b6e26430-dad2-492a-bdb9-f5ed7cb2929f'::uuid,NULL,NULL,91,614,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.921546+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('b7670b62-d431-41af-a069-17fc1a6db7bd'::uuid,'56576a1d-5b81-4e06-824c-2a0e45e5a305'::uuid,90,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
54	 	 Chapter 2
The Power of Loose Coupling
Design Principle
Strive for loosely coupled designs
between objects that interact.
When two objects are loosely coupled, they can interact, but they typically have very little knowledge
of each other. As we’re going to see, loosely coupled designs often give us a lot of flexibility (more
on that in a bit). And, as it turns out, the Observer Pattern is a great example of loose coupling.
Let’s walk through all the ways the pattern achieves loose coupling:
Loosely coupled designs allow us to build flexible OO
systems that can handle change because they minimize
the interdependency between objects.
How many
different kinds
of change can you
identify here?
First, the only thing the subject knows about an observer is that it
implements a certain interface (the Observer interface). It doesn’t need to
know the concrete class of the observer, what it does, or anything else about it.
We can add new observers at any time. Because the only thing the subject depends
on is a list of objects that implement the Observer interface, we can add new observers
whenever we want. In fact, we can replace any observer at runtime with another observer
and the subject will keep purring along. Likewise, we can remove observers at any time.
We never need to modify the subject to add new types of observers. Let’s say
we have a new concrete class come along that needs to be an observer. We don’t need
to make any changes to the subject to accommodate the new class type; all we have
to do is implement the Observer interface in the new class and register as an observer.
The subject doesn’t care; it will deliver notifications to any object that implements the
Observer interface.
We can reuse subjects or observers independently of each other. If we have
another use for a subject or an observer, we can easily reuse them because the two aren’t
tightly coupled.
Changes to either the subject or an observer will not affect the other.
Because the two are loosely coupled, we are free to make changes to either, as long as the
objects still meet their obligations to implement the Subject or Observer interfaces.
Look! We have a new
Design Principle!
loose coupling

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces the design principle: "Strive for loosely coupled designs between objects that interact." It explains how loose coupling minimizes interdependency, creating flexible systems that handle change better. Using the Observer Pattern as an example, it highlights that the subject only knows the observer''s interface, not its concrete class. This allows for adding, removing, or replacing observers at runtime without modifying the subject. Consequently, subjects and observers can be reused independently, and changes to one component don''t break the other as long as interface contracts are maintained.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the primary Design Principle discussed in this text regarding object interaction?
2. In the context of the Observer Pattern, why doesn''t the Subject need to know the concrete class of its Observers?
3. How does loose coupling allow for the addition of new observer types without modifying the Subject code?
4. Under what condition can changes be made to a subject or an observer without affecting the other in a loosely coupled design?','04bb2b27-f3ff-442a-9660-3a250237dcc2'::uuid,NULL,NULL,92,834,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.921673+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('d3fb1d06-cfdb-417e-9732-78cfbeb88fc5'::uuid,'56576a1d-5b81-4e06-824c-2a0e45e5a305'::uuid,91,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
