1
Meanwhile, back at Weather-O-Rama
There’s another way of handling the data in the Subject: we can rely on the
Observers to pull it from the Subject as needed. Right now, when the Subject’s data
changes, we push the new values for temperature, humidity, and pressure to the
Observers, by passing that data in the call to update().
Let’s set things up so that when an Observer is notified of a change, it calls getter
methods on the Subject to pull the values it needs.
To switch to using pull, we need to make a few small changes to our existing code.
Here we’re using the
Subject’s getter methods
that were supplied with
the code in WeatherData
from Weather-O-Rama.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains how to transition from a ''push'' model to a ''pull'' model within the Observer Pattern. Instead of the Subject sending all data to Observers via the update() method''s parameters, the Subject simply notifies the Observers that a change occurred. The Observers then use getter methods on the Subject to retrieve only the specific data they need. This change involves updating the notifyObservers() method, the Observer interface signature, and the concrete implementations of the update() method in each Observer.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the primary difference between ''pushing'' data and ''pulling'' data in the Observer Pattern?
2. How does the signature of the update() method in the Observer interface change when switching to a pull-based model?
3. In the pull-based approach, what must the Subject provide so that Observers can retrieve the necessary information?
4. Why might a developer choose to use a pull-based notification instead of passing all data through the update() method?','31811338-f034-4153-ac0d-09bb96cc4e12'::uuid,NULL,NULL,107,654,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.923328+07');
