This segment explains the functional mechanics of the Decorator Pattern using a coffee order example. It demonstrates how behavior, such as cost calculation, is achieved through delegation: an outermost decorator calls the next wrapped object until the base component is reached, summing values along the way. Key characteristics include: decorators share the same supertype as the objects they wrap (allowing substitution), multiple decorators can be applied, and behavior is added before or after delegation. Decoration happens dynamically at runtime, offering high flexibility.

