106	 	 Chapter 3
exercise solutions
Write the cost() methods for the following classes
(pseudo-Java is okay). Here’s our solution:
public class Beverage {
// declare instance variables for milkCost,
// soyCost, mochaCost, and whipCost, and
// getters and setters for milk, soy, mocha
// and whip.
public double cost() {
double condimentCost = 0.0;
if (hasMilk()) {
condimentCost += milkCost;
}
if (hasSoy()) {
condimentCost += soyCost;
}
if (hasMocha()) {
condimentCost += mochaCost;
}
if (hasWhip()) {
condimentCost += whipCost;
}
return condimentCost;
}
}
public class DarkRoast extends Beverage {
public DarkRoast() {
description = "Most Excellent Dark Roast";
}
public double cost() {
return 1.99 + super.cost();
}
}

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment demonstrates an inheritance-based approach to calculating costs where a base class (Beverage) uses boolean flags to track condiments. The cost() method in the base class sums up these costs, while subclasses like DarkRoast call super.cost() to add their specific base price to the condiment total. This serves as a contrast to the Decorator Pattern, highlighting a design where the base class must know about every possible condiment, leading to less flexible code when new ingredients are added.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
