108    Chapter 3
exercise solutions
public class Soy extends CondimentDecorator {
public Soy(Beverage beverage) {
this.beverage = beverage;
}
public String getDescription() {
return beverage.getDescription() + ", Soy";
}
public double cost() {
double cost = beverage.cost();
if (beverage.getSize() == Size.TALL) {
cost += .10;
} else if (beverage.getSize() == Size.GRANDE) {
cost += .15;
} else if (beverage.getSize() == Size.VENTI) {
cost += .20;
}
return cost;
}
}
Our friends at Starbuzz have introduced sizes to their menu. You can now order
a coffee in tall, grande, and venti sizes (translation: small, medium, and large).
Starbuzz saw this as an intrinsic part of the coffee class, so they’ve added two
methods to the Beverage class: setSize() and getSize().  They’d also like for the
condiments to be charged according to size, so for instance, Soy costs 10¢, 15¢,
and 20¢, respectively, for tall, grande, and venti coffees.
How would you alter the decorator classes to handle this change in
requirements? Here’s our solution.
Here we get the size (which
propagates all the way to the
concrete beverage) and then
add the appropriate cost.
public abstract class CondimentDecorator extends Beverage {
public Beverage beverage;
public abstract String getDescription();
public Size getSize() {
return beverage.getSize();
}
}
We added a method, getSize(), for
the decorators that simply returns
the size of the beverage.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment illustrates how to adapt the Decorator Pattern to handle new business requirements, specifically variable pricing based on beverage size (Tall, Grande, Venti). By adding getSize() and setSize() to the base Beverage class and implementing a delegation method in CondimentDecorator, decorators like Soy can retrieve the size of the wrapped object. This allows the cost() method to dynamically calculate additional charges based on the specific size, demonstrating the pattern''s flexibility in extending functionality without changing the core object''s structure.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. How does the Soy decorator determine the size of the beverage it is wrapping to calculate the correct cost?
2. Why was the getSize() method added to the CondimentDecorator abstract class instead of just the concrete Soy class?
3. What are the three specific cost increments defined for the Soy condiment based on the beverage size?
4. In this implementation of the Decorator Pattern, where is the actual size state stored and how does the decorator access it?','9f2b4e74-d97e-416c-b976-7a2664e74285'::uuid,NULL,NULL,146,632,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.926933+07');
