110	 	 Chapter 4
thinking about new
Yes, when you use the new operator you are certainly instantiating
a concrete class, so that’s definitely an implementation and not an
interface. And you make a good observation: that tying your code to
a concrete class can make it more fragile and less flexible.
Duck duck;
if (picnic) {
duck = new MallardDuck();
} else if (hunting) {
duck = new DecoyDuck();
} else if (inBathTub) {
duck = new RubberDuck();
}
Here we’ve got several concrete classes being instantiated, and the
decision of which to instantiate is made at runtime depending on
some set of conditions.
When you see code like this, you know that when it comes time for
changes or extensions, you’ll have to reopen this code and examine
what needs to be added (or deleted). Often this kind of code ends
up in several parts of the application, making maintenance and
updates more difficult and error-prone.
Duck duck = new MallardDuck();
We want to use abstact types
to keep code flexible.
But we have to create an
instance of a concrete class!
When we have a whole set of related concrete classes, often we end up
writing code like this:
We have a bunch of different
duck classes, and we don’t
know until runtime which one
we need to instantiate.
When you see “new,” think “concrete.”
Okay, it’s been three chapters and you
still haven’t answered my question about
new. We aren’t supposed to program to an
implementation, but every time I use new,
that’s exactly what I’m doing, right?

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment discusses the inherent conflict between the OO principle of programming to interfaces and the necessity of using the ''new'' operator. It explains that using ''new'' ties code to concrete classes, making it fragile and resistant to change. When multiple concrete classes are instantiated based on runtime conditions, the code must be reopened and modified whenever types are added or removed. This leads to maintenance challenges and potential errors, highlighting the need for a better way to handle object instantiation.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. Why does the author state that tying code to a concrete class makes it ''fragile''?
2. What is the primary maintenance problem associated with code that instantiates concrete classes based on runtime conditions?
3. According to the text, what should programmers think of whenever they see the ''new'' keyword?
4. How does the use of the ''new'' operator conflict with the goal of using abstract types for flexibility?','98e41755-c879-44b5-a6aa-6e199ff46c90'::uuid,NULL,NULL,148,625,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.92708+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('ec6736f1-54e1-4652-8123-9bf82ea3691c'::uuid,'56576a1d-5b81-4e06-824c-2a0e45e5a305'::uuid,146,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
Technically there’s nothing wrong with the new operator. After all, it’s a fundamental part of most modern object-oriented languages. The real culprit is our old friend CHANGE and how change impacts our use of new. By coding to an interface, you know you can insulate yourself from many of the changes that might happen to a system down the road. Why? If your code is written to an interface, then it will work with any new classes implementing that interface through polymorphism. However, when you have code that makes use of lots of concrete classes, you’re looking for trouble because that code may have to be changed as new concrete classes are added. So, in other words, your code will not be “closed for modification.” To extend your code with new concrete types, you’ll have to reopen it. So what can you do? It’s times like these that you can fall back on OO design principles to look for clues. Remember, our first principle deals with change and guides us to identify the aspects that vary and separate them from what stays the same. What’s wrong with “new”? But you have to create an object at some point, and Java only gives us one way to create an object, right? So what gives? How might you take all the parts of your application that instantiate concrete classes and separate or encapsulate them from the rest of your application?

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explores the design issues associated with using the ''new'' operator to instantiate concrete classes. While fundamental to OOP, direct instantiation creates tight coupling, making code fragile when requirements change. By coding to an interface, developers can leverage polymorphism to insulate the system from change. The text emphasizes the ''open-closed principle'' and suggests that parts of an application that vary—specifically object instantiation—should be encapsulated to ensure the code is open for extension but closed for modification.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. Why does using ''new'' with concrete classes make code less flexible and harder to maintain?
2. How does coding to an interface help insulate a system from future changes?
3. What does it mean for code to be ''closed for modification'' in the context of this segment?
4. What core OO design principle should be applied to handle the parts of an application that vary, such as object instantiation?','01074cf2-c095-4c7c-9e31-2d92ebc10c89'::uuid,NULL,NULL,149,592,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.927167+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('2d0a9866-520f-4a33-9afc-53118a5cb8f7'::uuid,'56576a1d-5b81-4e06-824c-2a0e45e5a305'::uuid,151,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
