}
public Pizza orderPizza(String type) {
Pizza pizza;
pizza = factory.createPizza(type);
pizza.prepare();
pizza.bake();
pizza.cut();
pizza.box();
return pizza;
}
// other methods here
}
Reworking the PizzaStore class
PizzaStore gets the factory passed
to it in the constructor.
And the orderPizza() method uses the
factory to create its pizzas by simply
passing on the type of the order.
Notice that we’ve replaced the new
operator with a createPizza method
in the factory object. No more
concrete instantiations here!
Now it’s time to fix up our client code. What we want to do is rely on the
factory to create the pizzas for us. Here are the changes:
First we give PizzaStore a
reference to a SimplePizzaFactory.
We don’t know about you, but we’re thinking New York, Chicago, and California style pizza factories (let’s
not forget New Haven, too).
We know that object composition allows us to change behavior dynamically at runtime (among
other things) because we can swap in and out implementations. How might we be able to use
that in the PizzaStore? What factory implementations might we be able to swap in and out?

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment demonstrates refactoring the PizzaStore class to use a SimplePizzaFactory. By injecting the factory through the constructor, the PizzaStore delegates the responsibility of object creation. This removes concrete instantiations from the orderPizza method, replacing ''new'' operators with a call to the factory''s createPizza method. This design utilizes object composition, which facilitates flexibility and allows the application to swap different factory implementations, such as those for New York or Chicago style pizzas, dynamically at runtime.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. How does the PizzaStore class receive a reference to the SimplePizzaFactory?
2. What specific keyword is replaced in the orderPizza method by using a factory?
3. What is the primary benefit of using object composition when handling different pizza factories?
4. According to the text, what are some examples of regional factory implementations that could be swapped into the PizzaStore?','857e5e09-f861-4c31-ade6-adc03d36a0c3'::uuid,NULL,NULL,154,573,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.927708+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('78bdd58c-6f93-4ad8-b3f6-1d85959669ed'::uuid,'56576a1d-5b81-4e06-824c-2a0e45e5a305'::uuid,152,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
you are here 4	 	 117
the factory pattern
The Simple Factory defined
The Simple Factory isn’t actually a Design Pattern; it’s more of a programming idiom.
But it is commonly used, so we’ll give it a Head First Pattern Honorable Mention.
Some developers do mistake this idiom for the Factory Pattern, but the next time that
happens you can subtly show you know your stuff; just don’t strut as you educate them
on the distinction.
Just because Simple Factory isn’t a REAL pattern doesn’t mean we shouldn’t check out
how it’s put together. Let’s take a look at the class diagram of our new Pizza Store:
Think of Simple Factory as a warm-up. Next, we’ll explore two heavy-duty patterns
that are both factories. But don’t worry, there’s more pizza to come!
This is the factory where we create
pizzas; it should be the only part
of our application that refers to
concrete Pizza classes.
This is the client of the
factory. PizzaStore
now goes through the
SimplePizzaFactory to get
instances of pizza.
SimplePizzaFactory
createPizza()
Pizza
PizzaStore
orderPizza()
ClamPizza
VeggiePizza
CheesePizza
PepperoniPizza
prepare()
bake()
cut()
box()
These are our concrete products. Each
product needs to implement the Pizza
interface* (which in this case means “extend the abstract Pizza class”) and
be concrete. As long as that’s the case,
it can be created by the factory and
handed back to the client.
This is the product of
the factory: pizza!
We’ve defined Pizza
as an abstract class
with some helpful
implementations that
can be overridden.
The create method is
often declared statically.
*Just another reminder: in design patterns, the phrase “implement an interface” does NOT always mean
“write a class that implements a Java interface, by using the ‘implements'' keyword in the class declaration.”
In the general use of the phrase, a concrete class implementing a method from a supertype (which could be a
abstract class OR interface) is still considered to be “implementing the interface” of that supertype.
Head First
Honorable
Mention
Pattern
Honorable
Mention

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces the Simple Factory, clarifying that it is a common programming idiom rather than a formal Design Pattern. It illustrates how the SimplePizzaFactory encapsulates object creation, serving as the sole point in the application that references concrete classes like CheesePizza or VeggiePizza. By using this factory, the PizzaStore (client) remains decoupled from concrete implementations, relying instead on the abstract Pizza class. This setup centralizes instantiation logic, making the code easier to maintain when pizza types or implementation details change.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the primary difference between a Simple Factory and a formal Design Pattern according to the text?
2. Why is the SimplePizzaFactory described as the only part of the application that should refer to concrete Pizza classes?
3. What are the requirements for a class to be considered a ''concrete product'' that can be created by the factory?
