Using this approach, we rely on the JVM to create the unique instance of the Singleton
when the class is loaded. The JVM guarantees that the instance will be created before
any thread accesses the static uniqueInstance variable.
Can we improve multithreading?
For most Java applications, we obviously need to ensure that the Singleton works in the
presence of multiple threads. But it’s expensive to synchronize the getInstance() method,
so what do we do?
Well, we have a few options...
1.  Do nothing if the performance of getInstance() isn’t critical
to your application.
That’s right; if calling the getInstance() method isn’t causing substantial overhead for your
application, forget about it. Synchronizing getInstance() is straightforward and effective.
Just keep in mind that synchronizing a method can decrease performance by a factor
of 100, so if a high-traffic part of your code begins using getInstance(), you may have to
reconsider.
2.  Move to an eagerly created instance rather than a lazily
created one.
If your application always creates and uses an instance of the Singleton, or the overhead
of creation and runtime aspects of the Singleton isn’t onerous, you may want to create
your Singleton eagerly, like this:
Go ahead and create an
instance of Singleton
in a static initializer.
This code is guaranteed
to be thread safe!
We’ve already got an
instance, so just return it.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment discusses two strategies for handling multithreading in the Singleton pattern. First, if performance overhead is not a concern, simple method synchronization is effective despite being potentially 100 times slower. Second, ''eager instantiation'' solves multithreading issues by creating the instance when the class is loaded. This leverages the JVM''s guarantee that the static instance is created before any thread can access it, removing the need for runtime synchronization and improving performance in high-traffic applications.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the primary performance drawback of synchronizing the getInstance() method in a Singleton?
2. How does eager instantiation utilize the JVM to ensure thread safety without manual synchronization?
3. In what specific situation should a developer consider moving away from a simple synchronized getInstance() method?
4. What is the difference between lazy instantiation and the eager instantiation approach shown in the code snippet?','f87459ca-ef00-4235-baf3-fedc024e58dc'::uuid,NULL,NULL,219,666,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.934294+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('9f44734e-66e1-418f-aa08-5aa07d746c0b'::uuid,'56576a1d-5b81-4e06-824c-2a0e45e5a305'::uuid,217,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
182	 	 Chapter 5
double-checked locking
public class Singleton {
private volatile static Singleton uniqueInstance;
private Singleton() {}
public static Singleton getInstance() {
if (uniqueInstance == null) {
synchronized (Singleton.class) {
if (uniqueInstance == null) {
uniqueInstance = new Singleton();
}
}
}
return uniqueInstance;
}
}
Check for an instance and
if there isn’t one, enter a
synchronized block.
Once in the block, check again and
if it’s still null, create an instance.
Note we only synchronize
the first time through!
The volatile keyword ensures that multiple threads
handle the uniqueInstance variable correctly when it
is being initialized to the Singleton instance.
If performance is an issue in your use of the getInstance() method, then this method of
implementing the Singleton can drastically reduce the overhead.
3.   Use  “double-checked locking” to reduce the use of
synchronization in getInstance().
With double-checked locking, we first check to see if an instance is created, and if not, THEN
we synchronize. This way, we only synchronize the first time through, just what we want.
Let’s check out the code:
Double-checked locking doesn’t work in
Java 1.4 or earlier!
If for some reason you’re using an old version
of Java, unfortunately, in Java version 1.4 and
earlier, many JVMs contain implementations of
the volatile keyword that allow improper synchronization for
double-checked locking. If you must use a JVM earlier than Java
5, consider other methods of implementing your Singleton.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains ''double-checked locking,'' an optimization for the Singleton pattern in multithreaded environments. By checking if an instance exists before entering a synchronized block, it ensures that expensive synchronization only happens during the first call. It highlights the necessity of the ''volatile'' keyword for thread safety during initialization. Additionally, it warns that this method is only reliable in Java 5 or later, as earlier versions have flawed ''volatile'' implementations that can cause synchronization errors.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. How does double-checked locking improve performance compared to synchronizing the entire getInstance() method?
2. What is the role of the ''volatile'' keyword in this Singleton implementation?
3. Why is it necessary to perform a second null check inside the synchronized block?
4. Why is double-checked locking considered unreliable in Java versions 1.4 and earlier?','840fe383-bec2-4880-beea-ba3993f19707'::uuid,NULL,NULL,220,625,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.934395+07');
