Loading the Invoker

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the formal structure of the Command Pattern by mapping the Diner analogy to technical components. It details the interaction between four key roles: the Client (who creates the Command), the Invoker (who triggers the Command), the Command object (which encapsulates actions), and the Receiver (who performs the actual work). The pattern ensures decoupling by having the Invoker call a generic execute() method without knowing the details of the actions or the Receiver involved.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the specific role of the Client in the Command Pattern?
2. How does the Command object encapsulate the relationship between actions and the Receiver?
3. What method does the Invoker call to trigger a request, and why is this significant for decoupling?
