=== SUMMARY ===
This segment examines violations of the Principle of Least Knowledge (Law of Demeter) using Java code examples. It illustrates how calling methods on objects returned by other methods—such as chaining calls or extracting components into temporary variables—increases coupling and dependencies. The text challenges learners to identify these violations in practice and points out that even standard Java commands like System.out.println() represent common violations of this design principle, highlighting that while principles guide us, they are often balanced against practicality.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. Why does the expression ''station.getThermometer().getTemperature()'' violate the Principle of Least Knowledge?
2. Does the second code example successfully follow the principle by using a helper method, or does it still contain a violation?
3. Why is the standard Java command ''System.out.println()'' considered a violation of this design principle?
