You’ve seen how to implement an adapter that adapts an Enumeration to an Iterator; now write
an adapter that adapts an Iterator to an Enumeration.
public class IteratorEnumeration implements Enumeration<Object> {
Iterator<?> iterator;
public IteratorEnumeration(Iterator<?> iterator) {
this.iterator = iterator;
}
public boolean hasMoreElements() {
return iterator.hasNext();
}
public Object nextElement() {
return iterator.next();
}
}
Notice we keep the
type parameter
generic so this will
work for any type
of object.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment distinguishes between the Decorator, Adapter, and Facade patterns based on their intent: Decorators add responsibility, Adapters convert interfaces, and Facades simplify them. It provides a practical implementation of an Adapter called IteratorEnumeration, which allows an Iterator to function as an Enumeration. This example illustrates how the Adapter pattern bridges incompatible interfaces by mapping method calls (like hasNext to hasMoreElements) while using generic type parameters to maintain flexibility across different object types.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the specific intent of the Facade pattern compared to the Adapter pattern?
2. How does the Decorator pattern treat the interface of the object it wraps?
3. In the provided IteratorEnumeration code, which method of the Iterator interface corresponds to the hasMoreElements() method?
4. What is the advantage of using a generic type parameter (Iterator<?>) in the IteratorEnumeration constructor?','f2796d09-3233-4de3-81a9-a5d51baa7707'::uuid,NULL,NULL,313,450,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.943725+07');
