The Hollywood Principle and Template Method
The connection between the Hollywood Principle and the Template Method
Pattern is probably somewhat apparent: when we design with the Template
Method Pattern, we’re telling subclasses, “don’t call us, we’ll call you.” How?
Let’s take another look at our CaffeineBeverage design:
prepareRecipe()
boilWater()
pourInCup()
brew()
addCondiments()
CaffeineBeverage
brew()
addCondiments()
Tea
brew()
addCondiments()
Coffee
CaffeineBeverage is our high-level
component. It has control over the
algorithm for the recipe, and calls on
the subclasses only when they’re needed
for an implementation of a method.
Tea and Coffee never
call the abstract class
directly without being
“called” first.
The subclasses are used simply to
provide implementation details.
Clients of beverages will depend
on the CaffeineBeverage
abstraction rather than a
concrete Tea or Coffee, which
reduces dependencies in the
overall system.
What other patterns make use of the Hollywood Principle?

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the relationship between the Hollywood Principle ("Don''t call us, we''ll call you") and the Template Method Pattern. In this design, a high-level component like the abstract class CaffeineBeverage controls the algorithm''s flow and calls upon low-level subclasses (Tea and Coffee) only when specific implementations are required. This approach prevents low-level components from calling high-level ones directly, thereby reducing system dependencies and ensuring the high-level abstraction maintains control over how and when steps are executed.

