The Iterator Pattern Structure

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the formal structure of the Iterator Pattern. It defines the roles of four key components: the Iterator interface for traversal, the ConcreteIterator for managing state, the Aggregate interface for standardizing collections, and the ConcreteAggregate for instantiating the appropriate iterator. This structure decouples the client from internal collection implementations, allowing for polymorphic code. The text also highlights a structural similarity to the Factory Method pattern, where a subclass (the ConcreteAggregate) is responsible for deciding which specific object (the ConcreteIterator) to create.

