3. Why does the Waitress no longer need to know the specific implementation (like PancakeHouseMenu) of each menu?
4. According to the text, what information was lost during this refactoring and how can it be recovered?','db198488-70a8-475e-953b-1ad6506381b7'::uuid,NULL,NULL,394,545,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.95267+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('428f05b8-c50f-4418-b5ea-099b3d45cb68'::uuid,'56576a1d-5b81-4e06-824c-2a0e45e5a305'::uuid,392,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
358	 	 Chapter 9
time to refactor
What do we need?
The time has come to make an executive decision to
rework the chef’s implementation into something that
is general enough to work over all the menus (and now
submenus). That’s right, we’re going to tell the chefs that
the time has come for us to reimplement their menus.
The reality is that we’ve reached a level of complexity
such that if we don’t rework the design now, we’re never
going to have a design that can accommodate further
acquisitions or submenus.
So, what is it we really need out of our new design?
•
We need some kind of a tree-shaped structure that
will accommodate menus, submenus, and menu
items.
•
We need to make sure we maintain a way to traverse
the items in each menu that is at least as convenient
as what we’re doing now with iterators.
•
We may need to traverse the items in a more flexible
manner. For instance, we might need to iterate over
only the Diner’s dessert menu, or we might need to
iterate over the Diner’s entire menu, including the
dessert submenu.
There comes a time when we
must refactor our code in order
for it to grow. To not do so would
leave us with rigid, inflexible code
that has no hope of ever sprouting
new life.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the necessity of refactoring the existing menu system to handle increasing complexity, specifically the introduction of submenus. The goal is to move toward a tree-shaped structure that can unify menus and menu items. The new design must support flexible traversal, allowing users to iterate over specific submenus or the entire hierarchy with the same convenience as previous iterator implementations. Refactoring is presented as a vital process to prevent code from becoming rigid and unable to accommodate future changes.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. Why is refactoring necessary for the current menu implementation at this stage?
2. What specific type of structure is required to accommodate menus, submenus, and menu items together?
3. What are the requirements for traversing items in the proposed new design?
4. What happens to code that is not refactored when complexity increases, according to the text?','615300e2-dff8-48b9-8d4c-619968966d81'::uuid,NULL,NULL,396,548,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.952857+07');
