m
Menus are nodes and
MenuItems are leaves.
We can represent
our Menu and
MenuItems in a
tree structure.

=== SUMMARY ===
The Composite Pattern allows developers to build tree structures to represent part-whole hierarchies. This pattern enables clients to treat individual objects (leaves) and groups of objects (composites/nodes) uniformly. In a menu application, menu items act as leaves and menus act as nodes. By using this pattern, complex nested structures like submenus can be managed easily, allowing operations like printing to be applied consistently across the entire hierarchy regardless of whether an element is a single item or a collection.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the formal definition of the Composite Pattern?
2. What is meant by a ''part-whole hierarchy'' in software design?
3. In the provided menu example, what is the difference between a ''node'' and a ''leaf''?
4. What is the main advantage of being able to treat individual objects and compositions uniformly?','2bcd590e-432d-4038-b2e8-c141318aff70'::uuid,NULL,NULL,398,727,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.953114+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('fd069868-6d07-4761-ba0e-539a32691912'::uuid,'56576a1d-5b81-4e06-824c-2a0e45e5a305'::uuid,395,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
The Composite Pattern allows us to build structures of objects in the form of trees that contain both compositions of objects and individual objects as nodes. Using a composite structure, we can apply the same operations over both composites and individual objects.  In other words, in most cases we can ignore the differences between compositions of objects and individual objects. Menus Submenu MenuItems We can create arbitrarily complex trees. And treat them as a whole... ....or as parts. Operations can be applied to the whole... ...or the parts. print() print()

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces the Composite Pattern, which organizes objects into tree structures to represent part-whole hierarchies. It explains that this pattern enables uniform treatment of individual objects and compositions. By abstracting the differences between single items (like menu items) and groups (like menus), developers can perform operations—such as printing—on the entire structure or specific sub-sections without needing to distinguish between their types, allowing for the creation of complex, nested object trees.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What specific structure does the Composite Pattern use to organize objects?
2. What is the main advantage of treating individual objects and compositions uniformly?
3. Based on the menu example, what are considered ''nodes'' and what are considered ''parts''?
4. How does the Composite Pattern simplify the execution of operations like print() across complex hierarchies?','9f2a33a5-24ea-4062-a7ac-dc6cdea54029'::uuid,NULL,NULL,399,384,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.953192+07');
