Q: Component, Composite, Trees? I’m confused.
A: A composite contains components. Components come in
two flavors: composites and leaf elements. Sound recursive? It is.
A composite holds a set of children; those children may be other
composites or leaf elements.
When you organize data in this way you end up with a tree structure
(actually an upside-down tree structure) with a composite at the root
and branches of composites growing up to leaves.
Q: How does this relate to iterators?
A: Remember, we’re taking a new approach. We’re going to
re-implement the menus with a new solution: the Composite Pattern.
So don’t look for some magical transformation from an iterator to a
composite. That said, the two work very nicely together. You’ll soon
see that we can use iterators in a couple of ways in the composite
implementation.

=== SUMMARY ===
The Composite Pattern utilizes a shared Component interface to allow clients to treat individual objects (Leaves) and compositions of objects (Composites) uniformly. This creates a recursive tree structure where Composites manage child components and Leaves implement specific behaviors. By using this pattern, complex part-whole hierarchies can be manipulated through a single interface. While it is a different approach from the Iterator pattern, the two are often used together to traverse and manage the resulting tree structures effectively.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the primary function of the ''Component'' interface in the Composite Pattern?
2. How does the relationship between Composites and Leaves create a recursive tree structure?
3. Why might a ''Leaf'' node inherit methods like add() or remove() even if it cannot have children?
4. In what way does the Composite Pattern allow a Client to handle individual objects and collections uniformly?','6c47068e-9056-4703-8938-f7f6c104f7c7'::uuid,NULL,NULL,400,737,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.953309+07');
