INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('9179c68d-034a-4f4c-95ca-53d7db0934a7'::uuid,'56576a1d-5b81-4e06-824c-2a0e45e5a305'::uuid,426,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
392	 	 Chapter 10
things get messy
The messy STATE of things...
Just because you’ve written your gumball machine using a well-thought-out
methodology doesn’t mean it’s going to be easy to extend. In fact, when you go back
and look at your code and think about what you’ll have to do to modify it, well...
final static int SOLD_OUT = 0;
final static int NO_QUARTER = 1;
final static int HAS_QUARTER = 2;
final static int SOLD = 3;
public void insertQuarter() {
// insert quarter code here
}
public void ejectQuarter() {
// eject quarter code here
}
public void turnCrank() {
// turn crank code here
}
public void dispense() {
// dispense code here
}
First, you’d have to add a new WINNER state
here. That isn’t too bad...
...but then, you’d have to add a new conditional
in every single method to handle the WINNER
state; that’s a lot of code to modify.
turnCrank() will get especially messy, because you’d
have to add code to check to see whether you’ve
got a WINNER and then switch to either the
WINNER state or the SOLD state.
❏   A.	 This code certainly isn’t adhering to the
Open Closed Principle.
❏   B.	 This code would make a FORTRAN
programmer proud.
❏   C.	 This design isn’t even very object-
oriented.
❏   D.	 State transitions aren’t explicit; they
are buried in the middle of a bunch of
conditional statements.
❏   E.	 We haven’t encapsulated anything that
varies here.
❏   F.	 Further additions are likely to cause bugs
in working code.
Which of the following describe the state of our implementation?
(Choose all that apply.)

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains why using constants and conditional logic to manage states makes code difficult to extend. Using the Gumball Machine as an example, it shows that adding a ''WINNER'' state requires modifying every action method, violating the Open/Closed Principle. This approach lacks encapsulation, hides state transitions within complex logic, and is prone to bugs. Ultimately, it highlights that a design based purely on conditionals is not truly object-oriented and fails to handle evolving requirements effectively.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. Why does the text claim that adding a new state like ''WINNER'' is difficult in the current implementation?
2. In what way does the existing code violate the Open/Closed Principle?
3. What is the specific problem with how state transitions are handled in this design?
