INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('d731e3ea-f600-485e-bdaf-797cdaa2aa80'::uuid,'56576a1d-5b81-4e06-824c-2a0e45e5a305'::uuid,489,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
you are here 4	 	 455
the proxy pattern
Well, we’ve seen how the Proxy Pattern provides a surrogate or
placeholder for another object. We’ve also described the proxy as
a “representative” for another object.
But what about a proxy controlling access? That sounds a little
strange. No worries. In the case of the gumball machine, just think
of the proxy controlling access to the remote object. The proxy
needed to control access because our client, the monitor, didn’t
know how to talk to a remote object. So in some sense the remote
proxy controlled access so that it could handle the network details
for us. As we just discussed, there are many variations of the Proxy
Pattern, and the variations typically revolve around the way the
proxy “controls access.” We’re going to talk more about this later,
but for now here are a few ways proxies control access:
ƒ
As we know, a remote proxy controls access to a remote
object.
ƒ
A virtual proxy controls access to a resource that is expensive
to create.
ƒ
A protection proxy controls access to a resource based on
access rights.
Now that you’ve got the gist of the general pattern, check out the
class diagram...
The Proxy Pattern defined
The Proxy Pattern provides a surrogate or
placeholder for another object to control access to it.
We’ve already put a lot of pages behind us in this chapter; as you
can see, explaining the Remote Proxy is quite involved. Despite that,
you’ll see that the definition and class diagram for the Proxy Pattern
is actually fairly straightforward. Note that the Remote Proxy is one
implementation of the general Proxy Pattern; there are actually
quite a few variations of the pattern, and we’ll talk about them later.
For now, let’s get the details of the general pattern down.
Here’s the Proxy Pattern definition:
Use the Proxy
Pattern to create a
representative object
that controls access
to another object,
which may be remote,
expensive to create, or
in need of securing.

=== SUMMARY ===
The Proxy Pattern defines a surrogate or placeholder object that acts as a representative to control access to a ''RealSubject''. This control can take several forms depending on the use case: a Remote Proxy handles network communication for distant objects, a Virtual Proxy manages resources that are expensive to instantiate, and a Protection Proxy enforces security based on access rights. Essentially, the proxy manages the interaction between the client and the target object to simplify logic or protect the resource.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the formal definition of the Proxy Pattern?
2. How does a Remote Proxy ''control access'' for a client?
3. What is the primary difference between a Virtual Proxy and a Protection Proxy?
4. Why might an object that is ''expensive to create'' benefit from using a Proxy Pattern?','40fa50d3-ce17-4d65-98e1-a224ec03e125'::uuid,NULL,NULL,493,707,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.962963+07');
