the paintIcon() method will paint the image, not the loading message.
...then it’s time to start retrieving it (in case you
were wondering, only one thread calls paint, so we
should be okay here in terms of thread safety).
Code Way Up Close

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains how a Virtual Proxy handles asynchronous image loading to maintain a responsive user interface. Because the ImageIcon constructor is synchronous and blocks until the image is fetched, the proxy spawns a background thread to perform the retrieval. It uses a ''retrieving'' flag to prevent redundant threads. Once the image is loaded, the proxy calls repaint() to notify the UI, shifting from displaying a ''loading'' message to the actual image.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. Why is the image loading process performed in a separate thread instead of the main UI thread?
