500    Chapter 12
duck decorator
public class QuackCounter implements Quackable {
Quackable duck;
static int numberOfQuacks;
public QuackCounter (Quackable duck) {
this.duck = duck;
}
public void quack() {
duck.quack();
numberOfQuacks++;
}
public static int getQuacks() {
return numberOfQuacks;
}
}
8
We’re going to make those Quackologists happy and give
them some quack counts.
How? Let’s create a decorator that gives the ducks some new
behavior (the behavior of counting) by wrapping them with a
decorator object. We won’t have to change the Duck code at all.
As with Adapter, we need to
implement the target interface.
We’ve got an instance variable
to hold on to the quacker
we’re decorating.
And we’re counting ALL
quacks, so we’ll use a static
variable to keep track.
We get the reference to the
Quackable we’re decorating
in the constructor.
When quack() is called, we delegate the call
to the Quackable we’re decorating...
...then we increase the number of quacks.
We’re adding one other method to the
decorator. This static method just
returns the number of quacks that
have occurred in all Quackables.
QuackCounter is a decorator.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains how to use the Decorator Pattern to add stateful behavior—specifically quack counting—to objects without modifying their original classes. By creating the QuackCounter class, which implements the Quackable interface and wraps an existing Quackable object, the system can intercept the quack() method to increment a static counter. This approach maintains interface compatibility while allowing global tracking of total quacks across all decorated duck instances, satisfying the requirements of the ''Quackologists'' without changing the underlying duck implementations.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
