514    Chapter 12
flock composites are observables too
What if a Quackologist wants to observe an entire flock? What does that
mean anyway? Think about it like this: if we observe a composite, then
we’re observing everything in the composite. So, when you register with
a flock, the flock composite makes sure you get registered with all its
children (sorry, all its little quackers), which may include other flocks.
Go ahead and write the Flock observer code before we go any further.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explores the integration of the Observer pattern with the Composite pattern. It explains that observing a composite object, such as a Flock, implies observing all individual elements contained within it. To achieve this, the Flock class must delegate observer registration to all its children, including any nested sub-flocks. This ensures that a single registration at the composite level allows an observer to monitor every ''quacker'' in the entire hierarchy, demonstrating how patterns can be combined to manage complex object structures.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
