Behavioral
Creational
Structural
Creational Patterns involve object
instantiation and all provide a
way to decouple a client from the
objects it needs to instantiate.
Each of these patterns belongs
in one of those categories.
Read each category description and
see if you can corral these patterns
into their correct categories. This is a
toughy! But give it your best shot and
then check out the answers on the
next page.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment explains the necessity of categorizing Design Patterns as their volume increases. By partitioning patterns, developers can more easily organize, search, and compare them. The primary classification scheme consists of three categories: Creational (focusing on object instantiation and decoupling), Structural (focusing on composing classes and objects into larger structures), and Behavioral (focusing on how objects interact and distribute responsibility). Readers are challenged to assign common patterns, such as Singleton and Observer, into these specific functional categories.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. Why is it beneficial to classify Design Patterns into different groups or categories?
2. What is the primary focus of ''Creational Patterns'' in software design?
3. How do ''Structural Patterns'' differ from ''Behavioral Patterns'' in their approach to class and object composition?
4. According to the text, what are the three distinct categories used in the most well-known classification scheme?','aa5bf3d8-e794-4bbb-abc4-9c4182a2f165'::uuid,NULL,NULL,613,603,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.975242+07');
