// use X-Y coords
// & complex age
// related calcs
}

=== SUMMARY ===
The Flyweight Pattern optimizes memory usage by reducing the number of runtime object instances. Instead of creating thousands of identical objects, it uses a single shared instance while centralizing the state for all ''virtual'' instances in a separate manager or client object. This is ideal when many instances can be controlled identically. However, a key drawback is that individual logical instances lose their independent behavior, as they all share the same intrinsic object structure while externalizing their unique state.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. How does the Flyweight Pattern achieve memory savings in an application?
2. In the provided example, what is the responsibility of the TreeManager versus the Tree object?
3. What specific condition must be met for a class to be a good candidate for the Flyweight Pattern?
4. What is a significant limitation regarding the independent behavior of objects when using this pattern?','1d4015e3-c244-448b-b08f-74fae2ac36c9'::uuid,NULL,NULL,643,508,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.978416+07');
INSERT INTO coaching_chatbot.lecture_knowledge_chunks (lecture_knowledge_chunks_id,lecture_knowledge_id,chunk_index,chunk_content,qdrant_point_id,start_time_seconds,end_time_seconds,page_number,token_count,created_at) VALUES ('9e7705de-96ec-4922-b2ed-a18ebca59834'::uuid,'56576a1d-5b81-4e06-824c-2a0e45e5a305'::uuid,639,'=== ORIGINAL CONTENT ===
606    Appendix
interpreter pattern
expression ::=  <command> | <sequence> | <repetition>
sequence ::= <expression> '';'' <expression>
command ::= right | quack | fly
repetition ::= while ''('' <variable> '')''<expression>
variable ::= [A-Z,a-z]+
Interpreter
Use the Interpreter Pattern to build an
interpreter for a language.
Remember the Duck Simulator? You have a hunch it would also
make a great educational tool for children to learn programming.
Using the simulator, each child gets to control one duck with a
simple language. Here’s an example of the language:
A scenario
Now what?
You’ve got a grammar; now all you need is a way to represent and
interpret sentences in the grammar so that the students can see the
effects of their programming on the simulated ducks.
Now, remembering how to create grammars from one of your old
introductory programming classes, you write out the grammar:
Turn the duck right.
Fly all day...
...and then quack.
A program is an expression consisting
of sequences of commands and
repetitions (“while” statements).
A while statement is just
a conditional variable and
an expression.
right;
while (daylight) fly;
quack;
A sequence is a set of
expressions separated
by semicolons.
We have three
commands: right,
quack, and fly.
The Interpreter
Pattern requires
some knowledge of
formal grammars.
If you’ve never studied formal grammars,
go ahead and read through the pattern;
you’ll still get the gist of it.

=== SUMMARY ===
This segment introduces the Interpreter Pattern, which is used to build interpreters for custom languages. Using a ''Duck Simulator'' as an educational example, it explains how a formal grammar defines the rules of a language, such as commands (right, quack, fly), sequences, and repetitions (while loops). The pattern provides a way to represent these grammar rules in code and interpret sentences to execute specific behaviors. It is particularly useful for implementing simple scripting or programming languages within an application.

=== REVIEW QUESTIONS ===
1. What is the primary purpose of the Interpreter Pattern?
2. In the provided Duck Simulator example, what are the three basic commands defined in the grammar?
3. How does the grammar define a ''sequence'' of expressions?
4. What foundational computer science concept is recommended for understanding the implementation of the Interpreter Pattern?','1b0d4bdf-8fb6-4eca-bfad-41b03ef142b4'::uuid,NULL,NULL,644,597,'2026-05-08 16:43:33.978526+07');
